{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1792421855645,"sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","part":{"id":"prt_154ab0198001o3UThNaDbnBqJB","messageID":"msg_154aaf609001Ndk7DAjOc9BxHH","sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","snapshot":"89d3537d5ad6810ae0a2936b565d60522c0d8748","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792421861137,"sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_8iYNzzR1KOvZvGt0xwia2958","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"idle\\|transcript\\|3 s\\|three seconds\\|session.idle\\|session idle\\|on_session_end\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md | head -40; echo \"=== playbook opencode\"; grep -n \"idle\\|transcript\\|kill\\|3 s\\|new message\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-pi-opencode.md | head -40; echo \"=== tests opencode plugin\"; grep -n \"session.idle\\|spawn\\|killer\\|kill\" .opencode/plugins/tests/session-relaunch.test.cjs | head -40","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/148-session-relaunch-deep-review"},"output":"29:Codex binds three repo hooks rendered from `hook-registry.json` into [.codex/hooks.json](../../../.codex/hooks.json). `prompt-seed.mjs` binds `UserPromptSubmit`: a `[session-relaunch]` prompt receives the claim-seed instructions, and a non-seed prompt runs the same threshold check as `context-nudge.mjs`, which binds `PostToolUse`. The Codex hook installer allows each script under one event only, so seed detection and the mid-session nudge are separate entry points over `shared.mjs`. The fill comes from the transcript rollout's latest `token_count` event: `last_token_usage.input_tokens` is the numerator and `cached_input_tokens` stays out because it is already inside that count. The window is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the event's `model_context_window`. The nudge reaches the model as `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`, the envelope the repo's other Codex adapters emit. `stop-arm.mjs` accepts only payloads whose `hook_event_name` is `Stop`. It resolves the target, arms a matching pending record, then spawns the killer with `--transcript <transcript_path>`. A spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next Stop restarts its killer. Codex exports no pid variable, so the killer target resolves through the same ancestor rule the CLI's running-work check uses. `skilled-loop codex` always starts Codex with `--no-daemon` because its shared app-server daemon keeps the environment of the session that started it. In a git worktree, Codex reads its `hooks.json` from the main checkout, not the worktree. The automatic hop needs the session-relaunch rows in the main checkout's file. Each of those three rows, like the three session-relaunch rows in the Claude settings file, carries the repo's hook-drift fallback: a missing adapter prints a `mk-hook-drift host=<host> event=<event> adapter=<script>` line on stderr and still returns an envelope the host accepts. Claude's fallback adds `mkHookDrift: true`. Codex omits that key, because it rejects unknown keys, and answers its Stop event with `systemMessage` instead of `hookSpecificOutput`, because it rejects that envelope on Stop.\n31:Hermes runs through the enabled `repo-guards` project plugin, so the operator changes nothing in the user config. The plugin bridges three events to the Node entry points under `hermes/`. `post_api_request` records each call's `usage` per session. `pre_llm_call` runs `context-nudge.mjs` and returns the seed text on a `[session-relaunch]` first turn or the handoff text once the fill crosses the threshold, appended to the turn's user message. `on_session_end` runs `session-end-arm.mjs` and accepts a missing event name from the bridge, but rejects a supplied name other than `on_session_end`. It arms a pending record for this session and detaches the killer with the Hermes process resolved by the ancestor rule. A killer spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next session end restarts its killer. Hermes fires `on_session_end` after every message, so the plugin keeps each session's recorded usage until `on_session_finalize` or `on_session_reset` clears it. The fill counts input plus cache read and cache write tokens, never output. The window is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the event's `model_context_window`. If the event omits that field, the plugin resolves the window from Hermes model metadata. The 200-thousand-token default applies only when neither source provides a window. The nudge state logs which source supplied the window.\n71:Claude's `context-nudge` handles `PostToolUse` and `UserPromptSubmit`. It reads the latest main-chain assistant usage from the Claude transcript and compares the fill ratio with the configured threshold. The default threshold is 0.80. One more nudge fires 0.05 above that level. Each level fires once. The adapter accepts session ids matching `[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}`. Transcript read errors produce no usage. Failed Claude settings reads are treated as absent.\n83:Stop matches the event session id to the stored record. A pending record stays pending when `transcript_path` is missing. The hook logs `missing-transcript-path` and returns. It also leaves the record pending while background tasks remain. Otherwise Stop arms the record and starts the detached killer.\n85:Pi checks usage at `message_end` and holds the nudge for `agent_end`, because the message that crosses the threshold usually carries the prepare call itself. At `agent_end` it re-checks the pending or fresh armed record and the hop limit, then sends the held text with `deliverAs: 'followUp'` and `triggerTurn: true`, so the nudge reaches the model without an operator turn. Its adapter finds `killer.cjs` by walking up from any repository subdirectory. `tsconfig.pi.json` includes the extension entry so the adapter is type-checked. OpenCode adds its active nudge to the system prompt on every request while this session has no pending or fresh armed record, and outside `skilled-loop` while the planned note file does not exist. It keeps a claim seed in the system prompt until the note leaves `open` or a new non-seed user message arrives, then arms the record on `session.idle`.\n91:Codex reads the latest `token_count` record in the rollout named by the payload's `transcript_path` and nudges through `additionalContext` on `PostToolUse`. Its `UserPromptSubmit` entry point handles seed prompts and runs the same nudge check, because Codex binds each script to one event. Its `Stop` adapter accepts only `hook_event_name: Stop` and passes `transcript_path` to the killer. Claude, Codex and Hermes write the armed state before starting the killer, so a killer spawn failure does not return the record to pending.\n93:In Hermes the `repo-guards` plugin keeps each session's `post_api_request` usage in memory, returns the context adapter's text on `pre_llm_call` for a seed first turn or a threshold crossing, and arms the matching record on `on_session_end`, which fires after every message. `on_session_finalize` and `on_session_reset` drop the recorded usage. When Hermes does not include a context window in the event, the plugin resolves it from Hermes model metadata. The Hermes killer runs without `--transcript`, so idle means the configured idle time has elapsed since it started.\n95:Every arm path restarts a killer for an armed record that has no live killer owner: Claude Stop, Codex Stop, Hermes session end, OpenCode `session.idle` and Pi `agent_end`. It does nothing while a killer owns the lock. A record left armed by a failed spawn is therefore recovered by the next arm event.\n97:The killer polls transcript mtime every 100 ms. When the transcript stays unchanged for `--idle-ms` (3000 ms by default), it confirms the target is a descendant of the loop and re-reads the record. It sends SIGTERM only when the record is still armed and below the hop cap. At or above the cap it unarms the record with reason `hop-cap`, logs `killer-hop-cap` and sends neither a signal nor a notification. The OpenCode plugin runs on Bun, so it starts the killer with `node` instead of `process.execPath`, which points to the OpenCode CLI. The `--loop-id` value must be greater than 1. If the record is no longer armed, the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`. If the target exits during the SIGTERM call, it logs `target-gone`.\n99:The idle deadline (`--timeout-ms`, 30000 ms by default) does not shorten the 5000 ms grace period after SIGTERM. If the idle deadline passes before the transcript is idle, or the target stays alive through the grace period, the killer disarms the record with reason `killer-timeout` and attempts a macOS notification with the manual claim command.\n101:A single failed transcript read is not a vanish. The killer gives up only when the transcript has stayed unreadable for 1 s without a break (`TRANSCRIPT_MISSING_GRACE_MS`). That give-up follows the timeout path: the record is disarmed with `killer-timeout` and the notification is attempted.\n103:The killer lock stores its owner pid, a creation timestamp and an expiry timestamp. The expiry is the idle timeout plus 5000 ms of signal wait, 3000 ms of notification time and a 60000 ms margin. A lock past its expiry, or a two-field lock from an earlier build that is older than 10 minutes, does not count as a live owner even while its pid exists, because that pid may by then belong to an unrelated process. A live owner or a lock younger than five seconds without a readable owner pid logs `killer-lock-held`. Taking over a stale lock first acquires a takeover mutex, the `<lock>.takeover` directory, whose `owner` file records the pid of the killer that holds it. The mutex is reclaimed only when that owner is dead. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s. The reclaim is an atomic rename. A live holder keeps exclusion only while the directory stays younger than the 10-minute hold bound, because a real holder holds the mutex for a re-check, an unlink and a create, never minutes. Past that bound the recorded pid counts as reused and the mutex is reclaimed. A killer that cannot acquire the mutex logs `killer-lock-held`. The mutex holder re-checks the lock, logs `killer-lock-stale`, removes the lock and retries once. Leftover `.stale-` takeover directories older than 30 s are swept under the mutex. The killer records the inode of the mutex directory it creates and, on release, removes the path only while that inode still matches, so it never removes a takeover directory it did not create. The killer releases the lock file the same way: it removes the file only while the recorded owner pid is its own, and logs `killer-lock-not-owned` when a contender has taken it over. An owner file that cannot be read fails the killer with `killer-error` and exit code 1. The exceptions are a non-directory at the mutex path, which has no owner file and is judged by age, and an owner read that fails with `ENOENT` or `EACCES`, which counts as no owner. If releasing the mutex fails after the takeover has finished, the killer logs `killer-mutex-release-error` with the error code. The takeover result stands: the killer keeps the lock it acquired and continues supervising.\n129:| disarmed | The killer ended the record without a relaunch. A session that never went idle before the deadline, a transcript unreadable for 1 s and a session still running after the SIGTERM grace period end with reason `killer-timeout`. A record whose hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` ends with reason `hop-cap`, and the killer sends no signal. |\n164:The killer CLI requires `--loop-id` and `--target-pid`. `--transcript` is optional: the Claude and Codex Stop hooks and the Pi arm pass it (Pi passes its session file when the path resolves). The Hermes arm and the OpenCode plugin do not. Without it, idle means the configured idle time has elapsed since the killer started. The loop id must be greater than 1 and match `SKILLED_LOOP_ID`. The defaults are `--idle-ms 3000` and `--timeout-ms 30000`. The killer polls every 100 ms. `--no-notify` defaults to off. Without it, a timeout attempts a notification. The Stop hook starts the killer without `--no-notify`.\n181:When prepare exits 3, start Claude through skilled-loop or use the printed manual claim command. At the hop cap, prepare writes no record, logs `prepare-declined reason=hop-cap`, and its first line reads `[session-relaunch] hop limit reached; continue in this session (auto-compaction remains the fallback)`. A corrupt relaunch JSON file is a decline with reason `no-file`. An unreadable Claude transcript yields no context usage, and unreadable Claude settings files are skipped as absent.\n183:If the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`, it found no armed record before signalling. `killer-hop-cap` means the record's hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS`, so the killer unarmed it with reason `hop-cap` and sent no signal. `killer-lock-held` means another killer owns the lock or is creating it. `killer-lock-stale` means the owner process is gone or the unreadable lock is older than five seconds. `killer-lock-not-owned` means the lock no longer named this killer's pid when it exited, because a stall past the expiry let a contender take it over, so the killer left the file in place for its new owner. `target-gone` marks a target that exited between the final liveness check and SIGTERM. The killer polls every 100 ms. The idle timeout defaults to 30000 ms and the separate post-signal grace period is 5000 ms. If either wait expires while Claude remains alive, the killer disarms the record and logs `killer-timeout`. A transcript that stays unreadable for 1 s also ends with `killer-timeout`. `killer-timeout-superseded` replaces it when a later prepare armed a different note during the wait. The killer then logs the `watched` and `current` note paths and neither disarms nor notifies, because the newer note still needs its own killer. A signal probe that fails with `EPERM` means the pid belongs to another user's process, so the killer treats its target as gone instead of signalling it. `killer-lock-held` also appears while another killer holds the `<lock>.takeover` mutex. If an armed record has no live killer, the next arm event starts one. A live killer that owns the lock blocks that restart by design. `killer-error` with exit code 1 means the takeover mutex's owner file could not be read, for example because the owner path is a directory. The killer stops before the takeover and the armed record stays armed for the next arm event. `killer-mutex-release-error` means the `<lock>.takeover` directory could not be removed after the takeover finished. The takeover result stands and the killer keeps the lock it acquired, so this line does not mean the handoff failed. A leftover mutex is reclaimed by the next takeover once its owner is dead, or once it has no owner file and is older than 30 s.\n223:|   `-- session-end-arm.mjs  (on_session_end bridge)\n=== playbook opencode\n8:  - \"skilled-loop pi\"\n9:  - \"skilled-loop opencode\"\n26:The adapters use different host events and seed argument forms while sharing handoff state and `skilled-loop`. A live run confirms the host delivers those events and the restarted runtime claims the prepared handoff.\n50:| session-relaunch-pi-opencode | Pi and OpenCode session relaunch adapters | Run five automatable checks and one live handoff in each runtime. | Carry this short task through a context handoff, then finish it in the new session. Tell me when you are ready. | Pi extension smoke -> OpenCode import smoke -> Pi adapter suite -> OpenCode adapter suite -> wrapper runtime suite -> live Pi tmux hop -> live OpenCode tmux hop | Automatable commands exit 0. The Pi smoke loads its extension with `-ne -e`. OpenCode import prints `function`. The wrapper tests show Pi name ordering and OpenCode `--prompt` without a name flag. Each live run arms the matching record and starts a fresh runtime with the claim seed. | Capture command output and exit codes. For each live run capture the tmux transcript, runtime log entries and handoff status from open through claimed. | PASS when every automated check exits 0 and both live sessions claim their prepared handoff after the matching wrapper relaunch. FAIL when an automated check fails, a runtime is mismatched or a live claim seed is not delivered. | 1. Confirm the adapter path and host smoke command. 2. Check adapter event logs and the matching runtime field in the pending record. 3. Check the killer result, hop limit and runtime-specific seed arguments. |\n78:./.skilled/node_modules/.bin/vitest run --config .skilled/hooks/vitest.config.ts .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/pi/session-relaunch.test.ts\n81:Evidence: Vitest reports the Pi adapter suite passed. The cases cover threshold nudges, seed injection, `agent_end` arming and `agent_end respawns one killer for an armed record whose killer is gone`.\n89:Evidence: Node reports the OpenCode adapter suite passed. The cases cover plugin import, events, transform injection, seed handling, idle arming and `session.idle respawns one killer for an armed record whose killer is gone`.\n94:node --test .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/skilled-loop.test.cjs\n109:| Pi | Run 1 (`loopId=5810`) | Pass: one counted hop. The second attempt reached the hop cap. | `2026-10-17T12:21:37.096Z prepare loopId=5810 status=pending sessionId=01a149c7-c583-71e9-b0aa-b4e377ac39a8`<br>`2026-10-17T12:22:29.438Z killer-signal targetPid=5853 signal=SIGTERM`<br>`2026-10-17T12:22:29.545Z relaunch loopId=5810 reason=ready`<br>`2026-10-17T12:37:39.364Z prepare loopId=5810 status=pending sessionId=01a149d0-4aa1-72c8-a9f3-c7a8ee155ba1`<br>`2026-10-17T12:38:35.528Z killer-signal targetPid=27665 signal=SIGTERM`<br>`2026-10-17T12:38:35.637Z decline loopId=5810 reason=hop-cap` | Sent `Continue the live test.` to start the nudge turn. After relaunch, answered Gate 3 with A and LOGIC-SYNC with option 1, claim without prepare. |\n110:| Pi | Run 2 (`loopId=66040`) | Pass. | `2026-10-17T14:07:07.786Z prepare loopId=66040 status=pending sessionId=01a14a18-1760-77cc-8fea-31b2ee97a2f8`<br>`2026-10-17T14:08:14.614Z killer-signal targetPid=66057 signal=SIGTERM`<br>`2026-10-17T14:08:14.746Z relaunch loopId=66040 reason=ready`<br>`2026-10-17T14:21:58.232Z decline loopId=66040 reason=no-file` | Withdrew the instruction to leave `spec_folder` empty and ran `/speckit:save` with the 002 folder. After relaunch, answered Gate 3 with A and the `/speckit:resume` menu with A. |\n111:| OpenCode | Run D (`loopId=18795`) | Pass. | `2026-10-17T12:55:14.333Z prepare loopId=18795 status=pending sessionId=ses_eb61236dbffeJIAGr440Q3hdVT`<br>`2026-10-17T12:55:27.400Z killer-signal targetPid=18858 signal=SIGTERM`<br>`2026-10-17T12:55:27.589Z relaunch loopId=18795 reason=ready` | Answered Gate 3 with A. After the claim, told the session not to prepare and to complete the task. |\n112:| OpenCode | Run E (`loopId=66034`) | Pass. | `2026-10-17T13:42:01.958Z prepare loopId=66034 status=pending sessionId=ses_eb5e7ae9cffe88hvn2m9fkmscW`<br>`2026-10-17T13:42:12.628Z killer-signal targetPid=66052 signal=SIGTERM`<br>`2026-10-17T13:42:12.820Z relaunch loopId=66034 reason=ready`<br>`2026-10-17T14:21:30.627Z decline loopId=66034 reason=no-file` | Answered Gate 3 with A. After the claim, told the session the test had no task and to complete. |\n113:| OpenCode | Run F (`loopId=49691`) | Pass. | `2026-10-17T16:32:59.469Z prepare loopId=49691 status=pending sessionId=ses_eb55200afffeBCSDET2x5MbmO4`<br>`2026-10-17T16:33:12.721Z killer-signal targetPid=49710 signal=SIGTERM`<br>`2026-10-17T16:33:12.884Z relaunch loopId=49691 reason=ready`<br>`2026-10-17T16:34:42.303Z decline loopId=49691 reason=no-file` | Before relaunch, the orchestrator allowed the continuity writer once. After relaunch: none. The seed carried the folder answer. The session claimed and completed without a question or reply. |\n115:| OpenCode | Run B (`loopId=36553`) | Not a hop: Bun set `process.execPath` to `opencode.exe`, so the spawn launched OpenCode instead of the killer. A second Ctrl-C interrupted the fresh session before it claimed the note. | `2026-10-17T11:54:16.759Z prepare loopId=36553 status=pending sessionId=ses_eb64a06c2ffe6wXkUEP2W7PJwK`<br>`2026-10-17T12:01:05.149Z relaunch loopId=36553 reason=ready`<br>No `killer-signal` line. | Manually exited after the killer failed to run. Sent a second Ctrl-C two seconds after the relaunch, interrupting the fresh session before the claim. |\n128:tmux_command=\"cd '$repo_root' && PATH='$repo_root/.skilled/bin':\\$PATH SESSION_RELAUNCH_THRESHOLD=0.05 SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS=100 SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS=1 skilled-loop pi\"\n133:Observe Pi's `message_end` nudge, the seed injection at `before_agent_start` and matching-record arming at `agent_end`. Pi only decides the nudge at `message_end` and holds the text until `agent_end`, which re-checks the record and the hop limit before sending it as a follow-up that starts a turn. Confirm the killer ends the current Pi process and `skilled-loop` starts Pi with the prepared claim seed. Confirm the receiving TUI claims that note and continues the task. The 2026-10-19 benchmark report for this scenario records the seed claims those runs made in the receiving TUI.\n143:tmux_command=\"cd '$repo_root' && PATH='$repo_root/.skilled/bin':\\$PATH SESSION_RELAUNCH_THRESHOLD=0.05 SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS=100 SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS=1 skilled-loop opencode\"\n148:Observe `message.updated` usage handling, `message.part.updated` seed capture and the system transform. The claim instructions stay in the system prompt for every request until the note leaves `open` or a new non-seed user message arrives, so an auxiliary request that consumes the first copy no longer drops them. Confirm a matching record arms on `session.idle`, the killer ends the current OpenCode process and the wrapper restarts it with `--prompt \"<seed>\"` and no name flag. Confirm the receiving TUI claims the note and continues the task. The 2026-10-19 benchmark report for this scenario records the claim and completion those runs reached. `session.idle` on TUI quit remains UNVERIFIED.\n169:| `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/pi/session-relaunch.ts` | Pi event adapter. |\n171:| `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/nudge.cjs` | Shared nudge and seed text. |\n172:| `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/context-usage.cjs` | Shared runtime usage and window calculations. |\n173:| `.skilled/bin/skilled-loop` | Runtime selection, environment export and relaunch arguments. |\n174:| `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/pi/session-relaunch.test.ts` | Pi adapter regression suite. |\n176:| `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/skilled-loop.test.cjs` | Wrapper runtime relaunch argument tests. |\n177:| `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/session-relaunch-cli.test.cjs` | Prepare runtime recording tests. |\n179:Provenance: `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/pi/session-relaunch.test.ts`.\n=== tests opencode plugin\n16:const PLUGIN_PATH = path.join(REPO_ROOT, '.skilled', 'plugins', 'session-relaunch.js');\n19:  '.skilled',\n77:  t.mock.method(childProcess, 'spawn', (...args) => {\n149:test('kill switch stops an active OpenCode nudge', async (t) => {\n154:  const sessionID = 'ses-active-kill-switch-0001';\n156:  await hooks.event(assistantEvent(sessionID, 'assistant-kill-switch', 90));\n243:      'node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/'\n396:test('kill switch suppresses an in-loop nudge before handoff preparation', async (t) => {\n397:  const sessionID = 'ses-loop-kill-switch-0001';\n441:    '.skilled',\n442:    'skills',\n485:    '.skilled',\n486:    'skills',\n891:  'session.idle arms a matching record and spawns the killer without a transcript',\n894:  const spawn = stubSpawn(t);\n908:  await hooks.event({ event: { type: 'session.idle', properties: { sessionID } } });\n913:  assert.equal(spawn.calls.length, 1);\n914:  const [, args, options] = spawn.calls[0];\n920:    assert.equal(spawn.unrefCalls.length, 1);\n925:  'session.idle respawns one killer for an armed record whose killer is gone',\n928:  const spawn = stubSpawn(t);\n929:  const loopId = 'opencode-loop-respawn-0001';\n930:  const sessionID = 'ses-respawn-session-0001';\n943:  await hooks.event({ event: { type: 'session.idle', properties: { sessionID } } });\n944:  assert.equal(spawn.calls.length, 1);\n946:  const stateDir = path.join(root, 'skilled-relaunch');\n949:    path.join(stateDir, `killer-${loopId}.lock`),\n952:  await hooks.event({ event: { type: 'session.idle', properties: { sessionID } } });\n953:  assert.equal(spawn.calls.length, 1);\n958:test('session.idle uses node under Bun and ignores killer spawn errors', async (t) => {\n960:  const spawn = stubSpawn(t);\n963:  const sessionID = 'ses-bun-killer-session-0001';\n975:  await hooks.event({ event: { type: 'session.idle', properties: { sessionID } } });\n978:  const [command, args, options] = spawn.calls[0];\n981:  assert.equal(path.basename(args[0]), 'killer.cjs');\n984:  assert.equal(spawn.children[0].listenerCount('error'), 1);\n986:    spawn.children[0].emit('error', new Error('node was not found'));\n991:test('session.idle logs a killer spawn error and leaves the record armed', async (t) => {\n993:  const spawn = stubSpawn(t);\n994:  const loopId = 'opencode-spawn-error-0001';\n","metadata":{"output":"29:Codex binds three repo hooks rendered from `hook-registry.json` into [.codex/hooks.json](../../../.codex/hooks.json). `prompt-seed.mjs` binds `UserPromptSubmit`: a `[session-relaunch]` prompt receives the claim-seed instructions, and a non-seed prompt runs the same threshold check as `context-nudge.mjs`, which binds `PostToolUse`. The Codex hook installer allows each script under one event only, so seed detection and the mid-session nudge are separate entry points over `shared.mjs`. The fill comes from the transcript rollout's latest `token_count` event: `last_token_usage.input_tokens` is the numerator and `cached_input_tokens` stays out because it is already inside that count. The window is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the event's `model_context_window`. The nudge reaches the model as `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`, the envelope the repo's other Codex adapters emit. `stop-arm.mjs` accepts only payloads whose `hook_event_name` is `Stop`. It resolves the target, arms a matching pending record, then spawns the killer with `--transcript <transcript_path>`. A spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next Stop restarts its killer. Codex exports no pid variable, so the killer target resolves through the same ancestor rule the CLI's running-work check uses. `skilled-loop codex` always starts Codex with `--no-daemon` because its shared app-server daemon keeps the environment of the session that started it. In a git worktree, Codex reads its `hooks.json` from the main checkout, not the worktree. The automatic hop needs the session-relaunch rows in the main checkout's file. Each of those three rows, like the three session-relaunch rows in the Claude settings file, carries the repo's hook-drift fallback: a missing adapter prints a `mk-hook-drift host=<host> event=<event> adapter=<script>` line on stderr and still returns an envelope the host accepts. Claude's fallback adds `mkHookDrift: true`. Codex omits that key, because it rejects unknown keys, and answers its Stop event with `systemMessage` instead of `hookSpecificOutput`, because it rejects that envelope on Stop.\n31:Hermes runs through the enabled `repo-guards` project plugin, so the operator changes nothing in the user config. The plugin bridges three events to the Node entry points under `hermes/`. `post_api_request` records each call's `usage` per session. `pre_llm_call` runs `context-nudge.mjs` and returns the seed text on a `[session-relaunch]` first turn or the handoff text once the fill crosses the threshold, appended to the turn's user message. `on_session_end` runs `session-end-arm.mjs` and accepts a missing event name from the bridge, but rejects a supplied name other than `on_session_end`. It arms a pending record for this session and detaches the killer with the Hermes process resolved by the ancestor rule. A killer spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next session end restarts its killer. Hermes fires `on_session_end` after every message, so the plugin keeps each session's recorded usage until `on_session_finalize` or `on_session_reset` clears it. The fill counts input plus cache read and cache write tokens, never output. The window is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the event's `model_context_window`. If the event omits that field, the plugin resolves the window from Hermes model metadata. The 200-thousand-token default applies only when neither source provides a window. The nudge state logs which source supplied the window.\n71:Claude's `context-nudge` handles `PostToolUse` and `UserPromptSubmit`. It reads the latest main-chain assistant usage from the Claude transcript and compares the fill ratio with the configured threshold. The default threshold is 0.80. One more nudge fires 0.05 above that level. Each level fires once. The adapter accepts session ids matching `[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}`. Transcript read errors produce no usage. Failed Claude settings reads are treated as absent.\n83:Stop matches the event session id to the stored record. A pending record stays pending when `transcript_path` is missing. The hook logs `missing-transcript-path` and returns. It also leaves the record pending while background tasks remain. Otherwise Stop arms the record and starts the detached killer.\n85:Pi checks usage at `message_end` and holds the nudge for `agent_end`, because the message that crosses the threshold usually carries the prepare call itself. At `agent_end` it re-checks the pending or fresh armed record and the hop limit, then sends the held text with `deliverAs: 'followUp'` and `triggerTurn: true`, so the nudge reaches the model without an operator turn. Its adapter finds `killer.cjs` by walking up from any repository subdirectory. `tsconfig.pi.json` includes the extension entry so the adapter is type-checked. OpenCode adds its active nudge to the system prompt on every request while this session has no pending or fresh armed record, and outside `skilled-loop` while the planned note file does not exist. It keeps a claim seed in the system prompt until the note leaves `open` or a new non-seed user message arrives, then arms the record on `session.idle`.\n91:Codex reads the latest `token_count` record in the rollout named by the payload's `transcript_path` and nudges through `additionalContext` on `PostToolUse`. Its `UserPromptSubmit` entry point handles seed prompts and runs the same nudge check, because Codex binds each script to one event. Its `Stop` adapter accepts only `hook_event_name: Stop` and passes `transcript_path` to the killer. Claude, Codex and Hermes write the armed state before starting the killer, so a killer spawn failure does not return the record to pending.\n93:In Hermes the `repo-guards` plugin keeps each session's `post_api_request` usage in memory, returns the context adapter's text on `pre_llm_call` for a seed first turn or a threshold crossing, and arms the matching record on `on_session_end`, which fires after every message. `on_session_finalize` and `on_session_reset` drop the recorded usage. When Hermes does not include a context window in the event, the plugin resolves it from Hermes model metadata. The Hermes killer runs without `--transcript`, so idle means the configured idle time has elapsed since it started.\n95:Every arm path restarts a killer for an armed record that has no live killer owner: Claude Stop, Codex Stop, Hermes session end, OpenCode `session.idle` and Pi `agent_end`. It does nothing while a killer owns the lock. A record left armed by a failed spawn is therefore recovered by the next arm event.\n97:The killer polls transcript mtime every 100 ms. When the transcript stays unchanged for `--idle-ms` (3000 ms by default), it confirms the target is a descendant of the loop and re-reads the record. It sends SIGTERM only when the record is still armed and below the hop cap. At or above the cap it unarms the record with reason `hop-cap`, logs `killer-hop-cap` and sends neither a signal nor a notification. The OpenCode plugin runs on Bun, so it starts the killer with `node` instead of `process.execPath`, which points to the OpenCode CLI. The `--loop-id` value must be greater than 1. If the record is no longer armed, the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`. If the target exits during the SIGTERM call, it logs `target-gone`.\n99:The idle deadline (`--timeout-ms`, 30000 ms by default) does not shorten the 5000 ms grace period after SIGTERM. If the idle deadline passes before the transcript is idle, or the target stays alive through the grace period, the killer disarms the record with reason `killer-timeout` and attempts a macOS notification with the manual claim command.\n101:A single failed transcript read is not a vanish. The killer gives up only when the transcript has stayed unreadable for 1 s without a break (`TRANSCRIPT_MISSING_GRACE_MS`). That give-up follows the timeout path: the record is disarmed with `killer-timeout` and the notification is attempted.\n103:The killer lock stores its owner pid, a creation timestamp and an expiry timestamp. The expiry is the idle timeout plus 5000 ms of signal wait, 3000 ms of notification time and a 60000 ms margin. A lock past its expiry, or a two-field lock from an earlier build that is older than 10 minutes, does not count as a live owner even while its pid exists, because that pid may by then belong to an unrelated process. A live owner or a lock younger than five seconds without a readable owner pid logs `killer-lock-held`. Taking over a stale lock first acquires a takeover mutex, the `<lock>.takeover` directory, whose `owner` file records the pid of the killer that holds it. The mutex is reclaimed only when that owner is dead. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s. The reclaim is an atomic rename. A live holder keeps exclusion only while the directory stays younger than the 10-minute hold bound, because a real holder holds the mutex for a re-check, an unlink and a create, never minutes. Past that bound the recorded pid counts as reused and the mutex is reclaimed. A killer that cannot acquire the mutex logs `killer-lock-held`. The mutex holder re-checks the lock, logs `killer-lock-stale`, removes the lock and retries once. Leftover `.stale-` takeover directories older than 30 s are swept under the mutex. The killer records the inode of the mutex directory it creates and, on release, removes the path only while that inode still matches, so it never removes a takeover directory it did not create. The killer releases the lock file the same way: it removes the file only while the recorded owner pid is its own, and logs `killer-lock-not-owned` when a contender has taken it over. An owner file that cannot be read fails the killer with `killer-error` and exit code 1. The exceptions are a non-directory at the mutex path, which has no owner file and is judged by age, and an owner read that fails with `ENOENT` or `EACCES`, which counts as no owner. If releasing the mutex fails after the takeover has finished, the killer logs `killer-mutex-release-error` with the error code. The takeover result stands: the killer keeps the lock it acquired and continues supervising.\n129:| disarmed | The killer ended the record without a relaunch. A session that never went idle before the deadline, a transcript unreadable for 1 s and a session still running after the SIGTERM grace period end with reason `killer-timeout`. A record whose hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` ends with reason `hop-cap`, and the killer sends no signal. |\n164:The killer CLI requires `--loop-id` and `--target-pid`. `--transcript` is optional: the Claude and Codex Stop hooks and the Pi arm pass it (Pi passes its session file when the path resolves). The Hermes arm and the OpenCode plugin do not. Without it, idle means the configured idle time has elapsed since the killer started. The loop id must be greater than 1 and match `SKILLED_LOOP_ID`. The defaults are `--idle-ms 3000` and `--timeout-ms 30000`. The killer polls every 100 ms. `--no-notify` defaults to off. Without it, a timeout attempts a notification. The Stop hook starts the killer without `--no-notify`.\n181:When prepare exits 3, start Claude through skilled-loop or use the printed manual claim command. At the hop cap, prepare writes no record, logs `prepare-declined reason=hop-cap`, and its first line reads `[session-relaunch] hop limit reached; continue in this session (auto-compaction remains the fallback)`. A corrupt relaunch JSON file is a decline with reason `no-file`. An unreadable Claude transcript yields no context usage, and unreadable Claude settings files are skipped as absent.\n183:If the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`, it found no armed record before signalling. `killer-hop-cap` means the record's hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS`, so the killer unarmed it with reason `hop-cap` and sent no signal. `killer-lock-held` means another killer owns the lock or is creating it. `killer-lock-stale` means the owner process is gone or the unreadable lock is older than five seconds. `killer-lock-not-owned` means the lock no longer named this killer's pid when it exited, because a stall past the expiry let a contender take it over, so the killer left the file in place for its new owner. `target-gone` marks a target that exited between the final liveness check and SIGTERM. The killer polls every 100 ms. The idle timeout defaults to 30000 ms and the separate post-signal grace period is 5000 ms. If either wait expires while Claude remains alive, the killer disarms the record and logs `killer-timeout`. A transcript that stays unreadable for 1 s also ends with `killer-timeout`. `killer-timeout-superseded` replaces it when a later prepare armed a different note during the wait. The killer then logs the `watched` and `current` note paths and neither disarms nor notifies, because the newer note still needs its own killer. A signal probe that fails with `EPERM` means the pid belongs to another user's process, so the killer treats its target as gone instead of signalling it. `killer-lock-held` also appears while another killer holds the `<lock>.takeover` mutex. If an armed record has no live killer, the next arm event starts one. A live killer that owns the lock blocks that restart by design. `killer-error` with exit code 1 means the takeover mutex's owner file could not be read, for example because the owner path is a directory. The killer stops before the takeover and the armed record stays armed for the next arm event. `killer-mutex-release-error` means the `<lock>.takeover` directory could not be removed after the takeover finished. The takeover result stands and the killer keeps the lock it acquired, so this line does not mean the handoff failed. A leftover mutex is reclaimed by the next takeover once its owner is dead, or once it has no owner file and is older than 30 s.\n223:|   `-- session-end-arm.mjs  (on_session_end bridge)\n=== playbook opencode\n8:  - \"skilled-loop pi\"\n9:  - \"skilled-loop opencode\"\n26:The adapters use different host events and seed argument forms while sharing handoff state and `skilled-loop`. A live run confirms the host delivers those events and the restarted runtime claims the prepared handoff.\n50:| session-relaunch-pi-opencode | Pi and OpenCode session relaunch adapters | Run five automatable checks and one live handoff in each runtime. | Carry this short task through a context handoff, then finish it in the new session. Tell me when you are ready. | Pi extension smoke -> OpenCode import smoke -> Pi adapter suite -> OpenCode adapter suite -> wrapper runtime suite -> live Pi tmux hop -> live OpenCode tmux hop | Automatable commands exit 0. The Pi smoke loads its extension with `-ne -e`. OpenCode import prints `function`. The wrapper tests show Pi name ordering and OpenCode `--prompt` without a name flag. Each live run arms the matching record and starts a fresh runtime with the claim seed. | Capture command output and exit codes. For each live run capture the tmux transcript, runtime log entries and handoff status from open through claimed. | PASS when every automated check exits 0 and both live sessions claim their prepared handoff after the matching wrapper relaunch. FAIL when an automated check fails, a runtime is mismatched or a live claim seed is not delivered. | 1. Confirm the adapter path and host smoke command. 2. Check adapter event logs and the matching runtime field in the pending record. 3. Check the killer result, hop limit and runtime-specific seed arguments. |\n78:./.skilled/node_modules/.bin/vitest run --config .skilled/hooks/vitest.config.ts .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/pi/session-relaunch.test.ts\n81:Evidence: Vitest reports the Pi adapter suite passed. The cases cover threshold nudges, seed injection, `agent_end` arming and `agent_end respawns one killer for an armed record whose killer is gone`.\n89:Evidence: Node reports the OpenCode adapter suite passed. The cases cover plugin import, events, transform injection, seed handling, idle arming and `session.idle respawns one killer for an armed record whose killer is gone`.\n94:node --test .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/skilled-loop.test.cjs\n109:| Pi | Run 1 (`loopId=5810`) | Pass: one counted hop. The second attempt reached the hop cap. | `2026-10-17T12:21:37.096Z prepare loopId=5810 status=pending sessionId=01a149c7-c583-71e9-b0aa-b4e377ac39a8`<br>`2026-10-17T12:22:29.438Z killer-signal targetPid=5853 signal=SIGTERM`<br>`2026-10-17T12:22:29.545Z relaunch loopId=5810 reason=ready`<br>`2026-10-17T12:37:39.364Z prepare loopId=5810 status=pending sessionId=01a149d0-4aa1-72c8-a9f3-c7a8ee155ba1`<br>`2026-10-17T12:38:35.528Z killer-signal targetPid=27665 signal=SIGTERM`<br>`2026-10-17T12:38:35.637Z decline loopId=5810 reason=hop-cap` | Sent `Continue the live test.` to start the nudge turn. After relaunch, answered Gate 3 with A and LOGIC-SYNC with option 1, claim without prepare. |\n110:| Pi | Run 2 (`loopId=66040`) | Pass. | `2026-10-17T14:07:07.786Z prepare loopId=66040 status=pending sessionId=01a14a18-1760-77cc-8fea-31b2ee97a2f8`<br>`2026-10-17T14:08:14.614Z killer-signal targetPid=66057 signal=SIGTERM`<br>`2026-10-17T14:08:14.746Z relaunch loopId=66040 reason=ready`<br>`2026-10-17T14:21:58.232Z decline loopId=66040 reason=no-file` | Withdrew the instruction to leave `spec_folder` empty and ran `/speckit:save` with the 002 folder. After relaunch, answered Gate 3 with A and the `/speckit:resume` menu with A. |\n111:| OpenCode | Run D (`loopId=18795`) | Pass. | `2026-10-17T12:55:14.333Z prepare loopId=18795 status=pending sessionId=ses_eb61236dbffeJIAGr440Q3hdVT`<br>`2026-10-17T12:55:27.400Z killer-signal targetPid=18858 signal=SIGTERM`<br>`2026-10-17T12:55:27.589Z relaunch loopId=18795 reason=ready` | Answered Gate 3 with A. After the claim, told the session not to prepare and to complete the task. |\n112:| OpenCode | Run E (`loopId=66034`) | Pass. | `2026-10-17T13:42:01.958Z prepare loopId=66034 status=pending sessionId=ses_eb5e7ae9cffe88hvn2m9fkmscW`<br>`2026-10-17T13:42:12.628Z killer-signal targetPid=66052 signal=SIGTERM`<br>`2026-10-17T13:42:12.820Z relaunch loopId=66034 reason=ready`<br>`2026-10-17T14:21:30.627Z decline loopId=66034 reason=no-file` | Answered Gate 3 with A. After the claim, told the session the test had no task and to complete. |\n113:| OpenCode | Run F (`loopId=49691`) | Pass. | `2026-10-17T16:32:59.469Z prepare loopId=49691 status=pending sessionId=ses_eb55200afffeBCSDET2x5MbmO4`<br>`2026-10-17T16:33:12.721Z killer-signal targetPid=49710 signal=SIGTERM`<br>`2026-10-17T16:33:12.884Z relaunch loopId=49691 reason=ready`<br>`2026-10-17T16:34:42.303Z decline loopId=49691 reason=no-file` | Before relaunch, the orchestrator allowed the continuity writer once. After relaunch: none. The seed carried the folder answer. The session claimed and completed without a question or reply. |\n115:| OpenCode | Run B (`loopId=36553`) | Not a hop: Bun set `process.execPath` to `opencode.exe`, so the spawn launched OpenCode instead of the killer. A second Ctrl-C interrupted the fresh session before it claimed the note. | `2026-10-17T11:54:16.759Z prepare loopId=36553 status=pending sessionId=ses_eb64a06c2ffe6wXkUEP2W7PJwK`<br>`2026-10-17T12:01:05.149Z relaunch loopId=36553 reason=ready`<br>No `killer-signal` line. | Manually exited after the killer failed to run. Sent a second Ctrl-C two seconds after the relaunch, interrupting the fresh session before the claim. |\n128:tmux_command=\"cd '$repo_root' && PATH='$repo_root/.skilled/bin':\\$PATH SESSION_RELAUNCH_THRESHOLD=0.05 SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS=100 SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS=1 skilled-loop pi\"\n133:Observe Pi's `message_end` nudge, the seed injection at `before_agent_start` and matching-record arming at `agent_end`. Pi only decides the nudge at `message_end` and holds the text until `agent_end`, which re-checks the record and the hop limit before sending it as a follow-up that starts a turn. Confirm the killer ends the current Pi process and `skilled-loop` starts Pi with the prepared claim seed. Confirm the receiving TUI claims that note and continues the task. The 2026-10-19 benchmark report for this scenario records the seed claims those runs made in the receiving TUI.\n143:tmux_command=\"cd '$repo_root' && PATH='$repo_root/.skilled/bin':\\$PATH SESSION_RELAUNCH_THRESHOLD=0.05 SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS=100 SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS=1 skilled-loop opencode\"\n148:Observe `message.updated` usage handling, `message.part.updated` seed capture and the system transform. The claim instructions stay in the system prompt for every request until the note leaves `open` or a new non-seed user message arrives, so an auxiliary request that consumes the first copy no longer drops them. Confirm a matching record arms on `session.idle`, the killer ends the current OpenCode process and the wrapper restarts it with `--prompt \"<seed>\"` and no name flag. Confirm the receiving TUI claims the note and continues the task. The 2026-10-19 benchmark report for this scenario records the claim and completion those runs reached. `session.idle` on TUI quit remains UNVERIFIED.\n169:| `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/pi/session-relaunch.ts` | Pi event adapter. |\n171:| `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/nudge.cjs` | Shared nudge and seed text. |\n172:| `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/context-usage.cjs` | Shared runtime usage and window calculations. |\n173:| `.skilled/bin/skilled-loop` | Runtime selection, environment export and relaunch arguments. |\n174:| `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/pi/session-relaunch.test.ts` | Pi adapter regression suite. |\n176:| `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/skilled-loop.test.cjs` | Wrapper runtime relaunch argument tests. |\n177:| `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/session-relaunch-cli.test.cjs` | Prepare runtime recording tests. |\n179:Provenance: `.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/pi/session-relaunch.test.ts`.\n=== tests opencode plugin\n16:const PLUGIN_PATH = path.join(REPO_ROOT, '.skilled', 'plugins', 'session-relaunch.js');\n19:  '.skilled',\n77:  t.mock.method(childProcess, 'spawn', (...args) => {\n149:test('kill switch stops an active OpenCode nudge', async (t) => {\n154:  const sessionID = 'ses-active-kill-switch-0001';\n156:  await hooks.event(assistantEvent(sessionID, 'assistant-kill-switch', 90));\n243:      'node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/'\n396:test('kill switch suppresses an in-loop nudge before handoff preparation', async (t) => {\n397:  const sessionID = 'ses-loop-kill-switch-0001';\n441:    '.skilled',\n442:    'skills',\n485:    '.skilled',\n486:    'skills',\n891:  'session.idle arms a matching record and spawns the killer without a transcript',\n894:  const spawn = stubSpawn(t);\n908:  await hooks.event({ event: { type: 'session.idle', properties: { sessionID } } });\n913:  assert.equal(spawn.calls.length, 1);\n914:  const [, args, options] = spawn.calls[0];\n920:    assert.equal(spawn.unrefCalls.length, 1);\n925:  'session.idle respawns one killer for an armed record whose killer is gone',\n928:  const spawn = stubSpawn(t);\n929:  const loopId = 'opencode-loop-respawn-0001';\n930:  const sessionID = 'ses-respawn-session-0001';\n943:  await hooks.event({ event: { type: 'session.idle', properties: { sessionID } } });\n944:  assert.equal(spawn.calls.length, 1);\n946:  const stateDir = path.join(root, 'skilled-relaunch');\n949:    path.join(stateDir, `killer-${loopId}.lock`),\n952:  await hooks.event({ event: { type: 'session.idle', properties: { sessionID } } });\n953:  assert.equal(spawn.calls.length, 1);\n958:test('session.idle uses node under Bun and ignores killer spawn errors', async (t) => {\n960:  const spawn = stubSpawn(t);\n963:  const sessionID = 'ses-bun-killer-session-0001';\n975:  await hooks.event({ event: { type: 'session.idle', properties: { sessionID } } });\n978:  const [command, args, options] = spawn.calls[0];\n981:  assert.equal(path.basename(args[0]), 'killer.cjs');\n984:  assert.equal(spawn.children[0].listenerCount('error'), 1);\n986:    spawn.children[0].emit('error', new Error('node was not found'));\n991:test('session.idle logs a killer spawn error and leaves the record armed', async (t) => {\n993:  const spawn = stubSpawn(t);\n994:  const loopId = 'opencode-spawn-error-0001';\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"idle\\|transcript\\|3 s\\|three seconds\\|session.idle\\|session idle\\|on_session_end\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md | head -40; echo \"=== playbook opencode\"; grep -n \"idle\\|transcript\\|kill\\|3 s\\|new message\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-pi-opencode.md | head -40; echo \"=== tests opencode plugin\"; grep -n \"session.idle\\|spawn\\|killer\\|kill\" .opencode/plugins/tests/session-relaunch.test.cjs | head -40","time":{"start":1792421861106,"end":1792421861135}},"id":"prt_154ab142b001DnSpYJCu5Mgjei","sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","messageID":"msg_154aaf609001Ndk7DAjOc9BxHH"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792421861548,"sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01_FwhAnFnxjwrwRpaDgTyu1353","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn \"idle\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md | head -30; grep -n \"idle-ms\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/148-session-relaunch-deep-review"},"output":".skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:45:    idleMs: 3000,\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:61:      '--idle-ms',\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:73:    if (argument === '--idle-ms') options.idleMs = parseInteger(value, argument);\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:148:    const idleAt = Date.now() + options.idleMs;\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:149:    while (Date.now() < idleAt && Date.now() < deadline) {\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:151:      const remaining = Math.min(idleAt - Date.now(), deadline - Date.now());\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:154:    return Date.now() >= idleAt\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:176:      } else if (Date.now() - unchangedSince >= options.idleMs) {\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:542:  const idle = await waitForIdle(options, deadline, signalTracker);\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:543:  if (!idle || signalTracker.received()) {\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:596:    // Start a fresh grace period after SIGTERM so the idle deadline cannot shorten shutdown.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:85:Pi checks usage at `message_end` and holds the nudge for `agent_end`, because the message that crosses the threshold usually carries the prepare call itself. At `agent_end` it re-checks the pending or fresh armed record and the hop limit, then sends the held text with `deliverAs: 'followUp'` and `triggerTurn: true`, so the nudge reaches the model without an operator turn. Its adapter finds `killer.cjs` by walking up from any repository subdirectory. `tsconfig.pi.json` includes the extension entry so the adapter is type-checked. OpenCode adds its active nudge to the system prompt on every request while this session has no pending or fresh armed record, and outside `skilled-loop` while the planned note file does not exist. It keeps a claim seed in the system prompt until the note leaves `open` or a new non-seed user message arrives, then arms the record on `session.idle`.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:93:In Hermes the `repo-guards` plugin keeps each session's `post_api_request` usage in memory, returns the context adapter's text on `pre_llm_call` for a seed first turn or a threshold crossing, and arms the matching record on `on_session_end`, which fires after every message. `on_session_finalize` and `on_session_reset` drop the recorded usage. When Hermes does not include a context window in the event, the plugin resolves it from Hermes model metadata. The Hermes killer runs without `--transcript`, so idle means the configured idle time has elapsed since it started.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:95:Every arm path restarts a killer for an armed record that has no live killer owner: Claude Stop, Codex Stop, Hermes session end, OpenCode `session.idle` and Pi `agent_end`. It does nothing while a killer owns the lock. A record left armed by a failed spawn is therefore recovered by the next arm event.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:97:The killer polls transcript mtime every 100 ms. When the transcript stays unchanged for `--idle-ms` (3000 ms by default), it confirms the target is a descendant of the loop and re-reads the record. It sends SIGTERM only when the record is still armed and below the hop cap. At or above the cap it unarms the record with reason `hop-cap`, logs `killer-hop-cap` and sends neither a signal nor a notification. The OpenCode plugin runs on Bun, so it starts the killer with `node` instead of `process.execPath`, which points to the OpenCode CLI. The `--loop-id` value must be greater than 1. If the record is no longer armed, the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`. If the target exits during the SIGTERM call, it logs `target-gone`.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:99:The idle deadline (`--timeout-ms`, 30000 ms by default) does not shorten the 5000 ms grace period after SIGTERM. If the idle deadline passes before the transcript is idle, or the target stays alive through the grace period, the killer disarms the record with reason `killer-timeout` and attempts a macOS notification with the manual claim command.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:103:The killer lock stores its owner pid, a creation timestamp and an expiry timestamp. The expiry is the idle timeout plus 5000 ms of signal wait, 3000 ms of notification time and a 60000 ms margin. A lock past its expiry, or a two-field lock from an earlier build that is older than 10 minutes, does not count as a live owner even while its pid exists, because that pid may by then belong to an unrelated process. A live owner or a lock younger than five seconds without a readable owner pid logs `killer-lock-held`. Taking over a stale lock first acquires a takeover mutex, the `<lock>.takeover` directory, whose `owner` file records the pid of the killer that holds it. The mutex is reclaimed only when that owner is dead. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s. The reclaim is an atomic rename. A live holder keeps exclusion only while the directory stays younger than the 10-minute hold bound, because a real holder holds the mutex for a re-check, an unlink and a create, never minutes. Past that bound the recorded pid counts as reused and the mutex is reclaimed. A killer that cannot acquire the mutex logs `killer-lock-held`. The mutex holder re-checks the lock, logs `killer-lock-stale`, removes the lock and retries once. Leftover `.stale-` takeover directories older than 30 s are swept under the mutex. The killer records the inode of the mutex directory it creates and, on release, removes the path only while that inode still matches, so it never removes a takeover directory it did not create. The killer releases the lock file the same way: it removes the file only while the recorded owner pid is its own, and logs `killer-lock-not-owned` when a contender has taken it over. An owner file that cannot be read fails the killer with `killer-error` and exit code 1. The exceptions are a non-directory at the mutex path, which has no owner file and is judged by age, and an owner read that fails with `ENOENT` or `EACCES`, which counts as no owner. If releasing the mutex fails after the takeover has finished, the killer logs `killer-mutex-release-error` with the error code. The takeover result stands: the killer keeps the lock it acquired and continues supervising.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:129:| disarmed | The killer ended the record without a relaunch. A session that never went idle before the deadline, a transcript unreadable for 1 s and a session still running after the SIGTERM grace period end with reason `killer-timeout`. A record whose hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` ends with reason `hop-cap`, and the killer sends no signal. |\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:164:The killer CLI requires `--loop-id` and `--target-pid`. `--transcript` is optional: the Claude and Codex Stop hooks and the Pi arm pass it (Pi passes its session file when the path resolves). The Hermes arm and the OpenCode plugin do not. Without it, idle means the configured idle time has elapsed since the killer started. The loop id must be greater than 1 and match `SKILLED_LOOP_ID`. The defaults are `--idle-ms 3000` and `--timeout-ms 30000`. The killer polls every 100 ms. `--no-notify` defaults to off. Without it, a timeout attempts a notification. The Stop hook starts the killer without `--no-notify`.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:183:If the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`, it found no armed record before signalling. `killer-hop-cap` means the record's hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS`, so the killer unarmed it with reason `hop-cap` and sent no signal. `killer-lock-held` means another killer owns the lock or is creating it. `killer-lock-stale` means the owner process is gone or the unreadable lock is older than five seconds. `killer-lock-not-owned` means the lock no longer named this killer's pid when it exited, because a stall past the expiry let a contender take it over, so the killer left the file in place for its new owner. `target-gone` marks a target that exited between the final liveness check and SIGTERM. The killer polls every 100 ms. The idle timeout defaults to 30000 ms and the separate post-signal grace period is 5000 ms. If either wait expires while Claude remains alive, the killer disarms the record and logs `killer-timeout`. A transcript that stays unreadable for 1 s also ends with `killer-timeout`. `killer-timeout-superseded` replaces it when a later prepare armed a different note during the wait. The killer then logs the `watched` and `current` note paths and neither disarms nor notifies, because the newer note still needs its own killer. A signal probe that fails with `EPERM` means the pid belongs to another user's process, so the killer treats its target as gone instead of signalling it. `killer-lock-held` also appears while another killer holds the `<lock>.takeover` mutex. If an armed record has no live killer, the next arm event starts one. A live killer that owns the lock blocks that restart by design. `killer-error` with exit code 1 means the takeover mutex's owner file could not be read, for example because the owner path is a directory. The killer stops before the takeover and the armed record stays armed for the next arm event. `killer-mutex-release-error` means the `<lock>.takeover` directory could not be removed after the takeover finished. The takeover result stands and the killer keeps the lock it acquired, so this line does not mean the handoff failed. A leftover mutex is reclaimed by the next takeover once its owner is dead, or once it has no owner file and is older than 30 s.\n61:      '--idle-ms',\n73:    if (argument === '--idle-ms') options.idleMs = parseInteger(value, argument);\n","metadata":{"output":".skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:45:    idleMs: 3000,\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:61:      '--idle-ms',\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:73:    if (argument === '--idle-ms') options.idleMs = parseInteger(value, argument);\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:148:    const idleAt = Date.now() + options.idleMs;\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:149:    while (Date.now() < idleAt && Date.now() < deadline) {\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:151:      const remaining = Math.min(idleAt - Date.now(), deadline - Date.now());\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:154:    return Date.now() >= idleAt\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:176:      } else if (Date.now() - unchangedSince >= options.idleMs) {\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:542:  const idle = await waitForIdle(options, deadline, signalTracker);\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:543:  if (!idle || signalTracker.received()) {\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:596:    // Start a fresh grace period after SIGTERM so the idle deadline cannot shorten shutdown.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:85:Pi checks usage at `message_end` and holds the nudge for `agent_end`, because the message that crosses the threshold usually carries the prepare call itself. At `agent_end` it re-checks the pending or fresh armed record and the hop limit, then sends the held text with `deliverAs: 'followUp'` and `triggerTurn: true`, so the nudge reaches the model without an operator turn. Its adapter finds `killer.cjs` by walking up from any repository subdirectory. `tsconfig.pi.json` includes the extension entry so the adapter is type-checked. OpenCode adds its active nudge to the system prompt on every request while this session has no pending or fresh armed record, and outside `skilled-loop` while the planned note file does not exist. It keeps a claim seed in the system prompt until the note leaves `open` or a new non-seed user message arrives, then arms the record on `session.idle`.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:93:In Hermes the `repo-guards` plugin keeps each session's `post_api_request` usage in memory, returns the context adapter's text on `pre_llm_call` for a seed first turn or a threshold crossing, and arms the matching record on `on_session_end`, which fires after every message. `on_session_finalize` and `on_session_reset` drop the recorded usage. When Hermes does not include a context window in the event, the plugin resolves it from Hermes model metadata. The Hermes killer runs without `--transcript`, so idle means the configured idle time has elapsed since it started.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:95:Every arm path restarts a killer for an armed record that has no live killer owner: Claude Stop, Codex Stop, Hermes session end, OpenCode `session.idle` and Pi `agent_end`. It does nothing while a killer owns the lock. A record left armed by a failed spawn is therefore recovered by the next arm event.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:97:The killer polls transcript mtime every 100 ms. When the transcript stays unchanged for `--idle-ms` (3000 ms by default), it confirms the target is a descendant of the loop and re-reads the record. It sends SIGTERM only when the record is still armed and below the hop cap. At or above the cap it unarms the record with reason `hop-cap`, logs `killer-hop-cap` and sends neither a signal nor a notification. The OpenCode plugin runs on Bun, so it starts the killer with `node` instead of `process.execPath`, which points to the OpenCode CLI. The `--loop-id` value must be greater than 1. If the record is no longer armed, the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`. If the target exits during the SIGTERM call, it logs `target-gone`.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:99:The idle deadline (`--timeout-ms`, 30000 ms by default) does not shorten the 5000 ms grace period after SIGTERM. If the idle deadline passes before the transcript is idle, or the target stays alive through the grace period, the killer disarms the record with reason `killer-timeout` and attempts a macOS notification with the manual claim command.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:103:The killer lock stores its owner pid, a creation timestamp and an expiry timestamp. The expiry is the idle timeout plus 5000 ms of signal wait, 3000 ms of notification time and a 60000 ms margin. A lock past its expiry, or a two-field lock from an earlier build that is older than 10 minutes, does not count as a live owner even while its pid exists, because that pid may by then belong to an unrelated process. A live owner or a lock younger than five seconds without a readable owner pid logs `killer-lock-held`. Taking over a stale lock first acquires a takeover mutex, the `<lock>.takeover` directory, whose `owner` file records the pid of the killer that holds it. The mutex is reclaimed only when that owner is dead. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s. The reclaim is an atomic rename. A live holder keeps exclusion only while the directory stays younger than the 10-minute hold bound, because a real holder holds the mutex for a re-check, an unlink and a create, never minutes. Past that bound the recorded pid counts as reused and the mutex is reclaimed. A killer that cannot acquire the mutex logs `killer-lock-held`. The mutex holder