{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792422009306,"sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_bzT0de59FHtuClM1yXK82934","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cat -n .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/context-preservation/session-relaunch-loop.md | sed -n '1,140p'; echo \"=== benchmark README row\"; grep -n \"session-relaunch-gate-exemptions\\|session-relaunch-final-code\\|session-relaunch-live-four-cli\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/benchmark/reports/README.md | head","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/148-session-relaunch-deep-review"},"output":"     1\t---\n     2\ttitle: \"Session relaunch loop\"\n     3\tdescription: \"Hands a session near its context limit to a fresh one through a structured handoff note and the skilled-loop wrapper.\"\n     4\ttrigger_phrases:\n     5\t  - \"Session relaunch loop\"\n     6\t  - \"Claude session relaunch\"\n     7\t  - \"Pi session relaunch\"\n     8\t  - \"OpenCode session relaunch\"\n     9\t  - \"Codex session relaunch\"\n    10\t  - \"Hermes session relaunch\"\n    11\t  - \"Cursor session relaunch\"\n    12\t  - \"Devin session relaunch\"\n    13\t  - \"/speckit:handoff session relaunch\"\n    14\t  - \"skilled-loop claude\"\n    15\t  - \"skilled-loop pi\"\n    16\t  - \"skilled-loop opencode\"\n    17\t  - \"skilled-loop codex\"\n    18\t  - \"skilled-loop hermes\"\n    19\t  - \"context handoff to a fresh session\"\n    20\tversion: 1.0.2.0\n    21\t---\n    22\t\n    23\t# Session relaunch loop (skilled-loop)\n    24\t\n    25\t---\n    26\t\n    27\t## 1. OVERVIEW\n    28\t\n    29\tShares a structured context handoff across seven runtimes through adapter nudges, a validated note and a wrapper relaunch. Claude Code, Pi, OpenCode, Codex and Hermes run automatically through their adapters. Cursor and Devin run on the `/speckit:handoff` command trigger only.\n    30\t\n    31\tAn automatic adapter asks for a note near its context threshold, validates and prepares it, then arms it at its host's turn-end event. The skilled-loop wrapper starts the selected runtime with a claim seed when the record is armed, fresh and below the hop cap.\n    32\t\n    33\t---\n    34\t\n    35\t## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n    36\t\n    37\t### Nudge, Prepare And Arm\n    38\t\n    39\tClaude's context-nudge handles PostToolUse and UserPromptSubmit. It reads the latest main-chain assistant usage and compares it with the configured threshold. The default is 0.80. One additional nudge fires 0.05 above that level. Each level fires once. The text opens with the measured fill as `Context is about N% full`. Once the ratio passes 1 it names the overflow as `past the configured window (over 100% full)` instead, so an over-wide window reads as overflow rather than as a percentage above 100. It then offers the shortcut before the steps: `If the remaining work fits in a few more steps, finish it instead and skip these steps.` Otherwise the handoff steps come first, before any other work. It accepts session ids matching [A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}. Transcript read errors produce no usage. Failed Claude settings reads are treated as absent while the resolver checks its next source or model default. If the operator says not to hand off, the nudge yields and the session keeps working. Auto-compaction remains the fallback.\n    40\t\n    41\tEvery adapter asks the same questions before it nudges. A pending record for this session silences the nudge. So does an armed one, until it passes its age limit, because the handoff work is already done. The limit is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_AGE_MIN`, read from the hook config file and then the environment, as the wrapper reads it. Past that limit the wrapper would decline the record as stale, so the nudge resumes instead of waiting on a relaunch that cannot happen. At the hop cap no adapter nudges, because a prepare there can only decline. A re-nudge also keeps the note path the session already has open: the shared planner returns that session's existing `status: open` note and only otherwise builds a timestamped path.\n    42\t\n    43\tBoth `prepare` and `claim` require `--session <id>`. Only Claude can use `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` instead. `CLAUDE_PID` supplies the runtime process id for the shell-child check only. Runtimes without a session-id variable generate an id with `uuidgen` and reuse it for the session.\n    44\t\n    45\tPrepare validates the note before checking whether the caller runs under skilled-loop. The nudge passes `--runtime <name>`, which must name one of the seven runtimes. Outside the loop, that value overrides the environment or Claude default and the CLI prints the selected runtime's manual claim command before exiting 3. Inside the loop, a supplied runtime must match `SKILLED_LOOP_RUNTIME` or prepare exits 2. A live child of the runtime process also exits 2 when its command name is one of `sh`, `bash`, `zsh`, `fish`, `dash`, `ksh`, `mksh`, `tcsh`, `csh`, `nu` or `pwsh`, with a leading `-` stripped so a login shell still matches. Any other child program is not detected. A successful prepare below the hop cap writes a pending record and exits 0. At the cap it writes no record: it prints a `hop limit reached` line that names auto-compaction as the fallback, then the manual claim command, logs `prepare-declined` with reason `hop-cap` and exits 3, leaving the note open for the operator's own claim. When a hop goes ahead, prepare marks each note already claimed by this session as `done`, adds `completed_at` and sets `superseded_by` to the new note's basename.\n    46\t\n    47\tThe note's Remaining work lists only unfinished task work. Note creation, continuity saving and prepare stay outside that section. Claude saves a bound packet with `/speckit:save`. Other runtimes pipe the save JSON to `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js --stdin --full-auto <spec-folder>`. Turn-end arming matches the event session id to the prepared record. Missing required payload data or background work leaves it pending. Otherwise, the adapter writes `status: armed` before starting the detached killer. A killer spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next arm event restarts its killer. Every arm path restarts a killer for an armed record with no live killer owner. It does nothing while one is alive.\n    48\t\n    49\t### Killer And Wrapper\n    50\t\n    51\tThe killer polls transcript modification time every 100 ms. Once the transcript is idle, it confirms that the target descends from the loop and re-reads the record. It sends SIGTERM only when the record is armed. Before a signal it re-checks the hop cap: a record at the cap is un-armed with reason `hop-cap`, logged as `killer-hop-cap` and left unsignalled with no notification, because no relaunch would follow and the manual claim is already the session's own instruction. Without a transcript it treats elapsed idle time since start as the idle signal. In OpenCode's Bun-hosted plugin, the detached killer runs with node because process.execPath points to the OpenCode CLI. A single failed transcript read does not end supervision. The killer gives up only after the transcript has stayed unreadable for 1 s without a break. That give-up re-reads the record first. When a later prepare armed a different note, it leaves that record alone and logs `killer-timeout-superseded` with the `watched` and `current` note paths. Otherwise it un-arms the record with reason killer-timeout and attempts the notification. Taking over a stale killer lock goes through a takeover mutex that records its owner pid. The mutex is reclaimed when that owner is dead. It is also reclaimed once it has outlived the longest hold a real holder needs. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s.\n    52\t\n    53\tSESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS and SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_AGE_MIN both accept zero. Zero max hops declines every relaunch with reason `hop-cap`. Zero max age declines an armed record once its age is greater than zero. The hooks, wrapper and killer append events to relaunch.log with fsync at mode 0600. A new event starts on its own line after a partial last line, so concurrent writers keep one event per line. A corrupt relaunch file is logged and treated as absent, so should-relaunch declines with reason no-file. The command also treats EACCES, ENOTDIR or EISDIR while reading the record as absent. Other command errors exit 2. Context transcript and settings read errors return absent values instead of escaping as exceptions.\n    54\t\n    55\tskilled-loop defaults to Claude when it receives no arguments or a prompt without a runtime name. The `cursor` arm launches the `cursor-agent` binary. It preserves the first launch arguments, including name flags. It scans name options only until --. Across hops, it carries model selection for all runtimes, the provider for Pi and Hermes, and Pi extension-selection flags. The wrapper applies the same value check to model, name, Pi extension and Pi or Hermes provider options. It leaves a value equal to `--` or starting with `-` in the first launch's arguments and does not carry it into later hops. Relative Pi extension paths are resolved against the wrapper's starting directory. Claude and Pi are the only runtimes with a launch-time name flag.\n    56\t\n    57\tA matching armed record is consumed only when it is fresh and below the hop cap. `spec_folder` carries three states: a repo-relative packet path, `none` when Gate 3 was answered D) Skip or empty when Gate 3 was never answered. The wrapper adds the path or `none` to the claim seed. The receiver resumes a packet path, skips Gate 3 for `none` and asks Gate 3 when the field is empty. The seed also carries the note path, so every adapter's claim instructions name the real file rather than leaving the `<handoff-path>` placeholder. Seeds printed by earlier builds spell the Skip suffix differently. The receiver still reads them as Skip. AGENTS.md states that a relaunch hop is the same session. The receiving runtime claims the note and continues only its task work. It never runs prepare on an already claimed note.\n    58\t\n    59\t`SESSION_RELAUNCH_THRESHOLD` controls when each session is nudged. Keep it well above a fresh session's startup fill plus its remaining work. Codex live runs chained hop after hop at `0.12` because each fresh session reached about 10% on startup alone. `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` caps completed relaunches. At the cap the chain ends on its own: prepare writes no record, the adapters stop nudging and the killer un-arms an armed record, so a re-nudged receiver is never killed for a relaunch that cannot start. Use a low hop cap for live checks.\n    60\t\n    61\t### Pi And OpenCode Adapters\n    62\t\n    63\tPi checks usage at message_end and holds the nudge text there rather than sending it, because the message that crosses the threshold usually carries the prepare call itself. agent_end re-checks the record and the hop cap, then delivers the held text with deliverAs: 'followUp', triggerTurn: true, so the nudge starts its own turn and needs no operator input. It captures a matching seed in before_agent_start and arms the Pi record on agent_end. Its killer-path resolver walks up from the current directory, so it can find killer.cjs from a repository subdirectory. tsconfig.pi.json includes the Pi extension entry for type-checking.\n    64\t\n    65\tOpenCode reads message.updated and message.part.updated. It pushes the active nudge on every request while this session has no handoff. Outside skilled-loop it keeps pushing until the handoff note exists. A matching claim seed stays in the system prompt on every request of the session until the note leaves the open state or a new non-seed user message arrives, because an auxiliary request can consume the first prompt. The seed enters through experimental.chat.system.transform. The relaunch record is armed on session.idle.\n    66\t\n    67\tThe wrapper's Pi relaunch carries provider and model selection together with -ne and each -e extension selection from the initial launch. In this repository, start Pi with -ne -e .pi/extensions/session-relaunch.ts because two installed extensions both register --fast. OpenCode is started with -m <provider/model>. The wrapper preserves that model selection across hops.\n    68\t\n    69\tIn the recorded live runs, OpenCode reported about 1M context tokens for openai/gpt-6-luna and Pi reported 272K. These reported window sizes differ by runtime.\n    70\t\n    71\t### Codex Adapter\n    72\t\n    73\tCodex binds three repo hooks rendered from hook-registry.json into .codex/hooks.json, one script per event because the Codex hook installer allows each script under one event only. `prompt-seed.mjs` on UserPromptSubmit returns claim-seed instructions for a `[session-relaunch]` prompt and runs the threshold check for any other prompt. `context-nudge.mjs` on PostToolUse runs the same check mid-session. `stop-arm.mjs` accepts only `hook_event_name: Stop`. It arms a matching pending record before spawning the killer with `--transcript <transcript_path>`, so a spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next Stop restarts its killer.\n    74\t\n    75\tThe fill reads the rollout's latest `token_count` event at `transcript_path`. The numerator is `last_token_usage.input_tokens`, which already contains the cached share, so `cached_input_tokens` is not added. The window is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the event's `model_context_window`. The nudge reaches the model as `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`. Codex exports no pid variable, so the killer target resolves through the ancestor rule the CLI's running-work check uses.\n    76\t\n    77\t### Hermes Adapter\n    78\t\n    79\tHermes runs through the enabled `repo-guards` project plugin, which bridges its plugin hooks to the Node entry points under `hermes/`. `post_api_request` records each call's `usage` per session. `pre_llm_call` returns the context adapter's seed text on a `[session-relaunch]` first turn or the handoff text once the fill crosses the threshold, appended to the turn's user message. `on_session_end` accepts a missing event name from the plugin bridge and rejects a supplied name other than `on_session_end`. It arms a pending record for this session before detaching the killer with the Hermes process resolved by the ancestor rule, so a spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next session end restarts its killer.\n    80\t\n    81\tHermes fires `on_session_end` after every message, so the plugin arms there and keeps each session's recorded usage until `on_session_finalize` or `on_session_reset` clears it. The fill counts input plus cache read and cache write tokens, never output. The window is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the context length in the event. If the event has no window, the plugin asks Hermes model metadata for the model window. The 200-thousand-token default is used only when neither source has a value. The chosen source is logged.\n    82\t\n    83\t### Command-Only Runtimes\n    84\t\n    85\tCursor ships on the command trigger because its evidence is source-only and `stop` is not wired in this repository's `.cursor/hooks.json`. Devin ships on the command trigger because its hook usage is unverified. On both, `/speckit:handoff` writes the note and runs prepare. Outside skilled-loop, prepare prints one runtime-specific manual claim command and exits 3. A prepared record stays pending because no arm event exists for these runtimes. When the runtime exits with a pending record, the wrapper also prints its manual claim command and preserves the runtime exit code. When a path contains `!`, the printed seed places the bang in single quotes outside the double quotes, as in `/tmp/han\"'!'\"doff.md`. Interactive Bash keeps the path intact when history expansion is on. If the wrapper cannot decode the record returned by `should-relaunch`, it prints `warning: could not read the relaunch record`, consumes the record and exits 1 without a manual claim command.\n    86\t\n    87\t---\n    88\t## 3. SOURCE FILES\n    89\t\n    90\t### Implementation\n    91\t\n    92\t| File | Layer | Role |\n    93\t|---|---|---|\n    94\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/claude/context-nudge.mjs | Handler | Reads hook input and returns threshold or claim-seed context. |\n    95\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/claude/stop-arm.mjs | Handler | Arms a matching prepared record on Stop and starts the killer. |\n    96\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/codex/prompt-seed.mjs | Handler | Answers `[session-relaunch]` seeds and runs the nudge check on UserPromptSubmit. |\n    97\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/codex/context-nudge.mjs | Handler | Runs the rollout-usage threshold check on PostToolUse. |\n    98\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/codex/shared.mjs | Shared | Shared payload, threshold and emit logic for the Codex entry points. |\n    99\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/codex/stop-arm.mjs | Handler | Arms a matching pending record on Stop and starts the killer with its transcript. |\n   100\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/hermes/context-nudge.mjs | Handler | Returns seed or handoff context for the plugin's pre_llm_call bridge. |\n   101\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/hermes/session-end-arm.mjs | Handler | Arms a matching pending record on on_session_end and starts the killer. |\n   102\t| .hermes/plugins/repo-guards/__init__.py | Handler | Bridges Hermes plugin hooks to the adapters and keeps per-session usage. |\n   103\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/pi/session-relaunch.ts | Handler | Sends context nudges, injects a relaunch seed, arms Pi records and resolves the killer path from repository subdirectories. |\n   104\t| .opencode/plugins/session-relaunch.js | Handler | Queues OpenCode nudge or seed text for a system transform and arms a matching record on idle. |\n   105\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/bin/session-relaunch.cjs | Script | Validates notes, prepares records and updates note status. |\n   106\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/context-usage.cjs | Shared | Reads Claude and Codex transcript usage, calculates Pi, OpenCode and Hermes token fills and resolves context windows. |\n   107\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/nudge.cjs | Shared | Builds the nudge and seed text, parses the note path, threshold and Gate 3 seed suffix. It also owns per-session level state. |\n   108\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/relaunch-core.cjs | Shared | Validates handoffs and handles relaunch and note state. |\n   109\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs | Script | Waits for transcript idle, gives up after a 1 s unreadable transcript, signals the target and guards stale-lock takeover. |\n   110\t| .skilled/bin/skilled-loop | Script | Wraps the seven runtimes and launches eligible relaunches with runtime-specific seed arguments. |\n   111\t| tsconfig.pi.json | TypeScript config | Includes the Pi extension entry so the adapter is type-checked. |\n   112\t| .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/hook-registry.json | Shared | Declares the Claude and Codex hook bindings rendered into each runtime's config. |\n   113\t\n   114\t### Validation And Tests\n   115\t\n   116\t| File | Type | Role |\n   117\t|---|---|---|\n   118\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/claude-adapters.test.cjs | Node test | Checks Claude nudge and Stop adapter behavior. |\n   119\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/codex-adapters.test.cjs | Node test | Checks Codex seed, threshold, arming and registration behavior. |\n   120\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/hermes-adapters.test.cjs | Node test | Checks Hermes usage, seed, window sources, arming and pid lookup. |\n   121\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/nudge.test.cjs | Node test | Checks the shared nudge text, levels and seed parsing. |\n   122\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/pi/session-relaunch.test.ts | Vitest | Checks Pi nudge, seed injection and agent-end arming. |\n   123\t| .opencode/plugins/tests/session-relaunch.test.cjs | Node test | Checks OpenCode event handling, transform injection and idle arming. |\n   124\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/context-usage.test.cjs | Node test | Checks transcript parsing, runtime usage calculations and window resolution. |\n   125\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/killer.test.cjs | Node test | Checks process supervision, timeout and lock behavior. |\n   126\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/relaunch-core.test.cjs | Node test | Checks note states and relaunch decisions. |\n   127\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/session-relaunch-cli.test.cjs | Node test | Checks prepare, claim, complete and CLI refusal paths. |\n   128\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/skilled-loop.test.cjs | Node test | Checks wrapper relaunch and per-runtime seed arguments with stub runtimes. |\n   129\t| .hermes/plugins/repo-guards/tests/test_repo_guards.py | Automated test | Checks the plugin bridge, including the relaunch context and arm paths. |\n   130\t\n   131\t---\n   132\t\n   133\t## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n   134\t\n   135\t- Group: Context Preservation\n   136\t- Canonical catalog source: feature-catalog.md\n   137\t- Feature file path: context-preservation/session-relaunch-loop.md\n   138\t\n   139\tRelated references:\n   140\t- [Resource map template](resource-map-template.md)\n=== benchmark README row\n27:| 2026-10-19 | [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/`](./2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/) | claude, pi, opencode session-relaunch with no Gate 3 answer | 3 PASS | **PASS** | `manual-testing-playbook` |\n28:| 2026-10-19 | [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/`](./2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/) | claude, pi, opencode, codex session-relaunch | 4 PASS | **PASS** | `manual-testing-playbook` |\n29:| 2026-10-19 | [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/`](./2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/) | claude, pi, opencode, codex session-relaunch | 11 PASS, 2 FAIL | **PASS (after fixes)** | `manual-testing-playbook` |\n","metadata":{"output":"     1\t---\n     2\ttitle: \"Session relaunch loop\"\n     3\tdescription: \"Hands a session near its context limit to a fresh one through a structured handoff note and the skilled-loop wrapper.\"\n     4\ttrigger_phrases:\n     5\t  - \"Session relaunch loop\"\n     6\t  - \"Claude session relaunch\"\n     7\t  - \"Pi session relaunch\"\n     8\t  - \"OpenCode session relaunch\"\n     9\t  - \"Codex session relaunch\"\n    10\t  - \"Hermes session relaunch\"\n    11\t  - \"Cursor session relaunch\"\n    12\t  - \"Devin session relaunch\"\n    13\t  - \"/speckit:handoff session relaunch\"\n    14\t  - \"skilled-loop claude\"\n    15\t  - \"skilled-loop pi\"\n    16\t  - \"skilled-loop opencode\"\n    17\t  - \"skilled-loop codex\"\n    18\t  - \"skilled-loop hermes\"\n    19\t  - \"context handoff to a fresh session\"\n    20\tversion: 1.0.2.0\n    21\t---\n    22\t\n    23\t# Session relaunch loop (skilled-loop)\n    24\t\n    25\t---\n    26\t\n    27\t## 1. OVERVIEW\n    28\t\n    29\tShares a structured context handoff across seven runtimes through adapter nudges, a validated note and a wrapper relaunch. Claude Code, Pi, OpenCode, Codex and Hermes run automatically through their adapters. Cursor and Devin run on the `/speckit:handoff` command trigger only.\n    30\t\n    31\tAn automatic adapter asks for a note near its context threshold, validates and prepares it, then arms it at its host's turn-end event. The skilled-loop wrapper starts the selected runtime with a claim seed when the record is armed, fresh and below the hop cap.\n    32\t\n    33\t---\n    34\t\n    35\t## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n    36\t\n    37\t### Nudge, Prepare And Arm\n    38\t\n    39\tClaude's context-nudge handles PostToolUse and UserPromptSubmit. It reads the latest main-chain assistant usage and compares it with the configured threshold. The default is 0.80. One additional nudge fires 0.05 above that level. Each level fires once. The text opens with the measured fill as `Context is about N% full`. Once the ratio passes 1 it names the overflow as `past the configured window (over 100% full)` instead, so an over-wide window reads as overflow rather than as a percentage above 100. It then offers the shortcut before the steps: `If the remaining work fits in a few more steps, finish it instead and skip these steps.` Otherwise the handoff steps come first, before any other work. It accepts session ids matching [A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}. Transcript read errors produce no usage. Failed Claude settings reads are treated as absent while the resolver checks its next source or model default. If the operator says not to hand off, the nudge yields and the session keeps working. Auto-compaction remains the fallback.\n    40\t\n    41\tEvery adapter asks the same questions before it nudges. A pending record for this session silences the nudge. So does an armed one, until it passes its age limit, because the handoff work is already done. The limit is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_AGE_MIN`, read from the hook config file and then the environment, as the wrapper reads it. Past that limit the wrapper would decline the record as stale, so the nudge resumes instead of waiting on a relaunch that cannot happen. At the hop cap no adapter nudges, because a prepare there can only decline. A re-nudge also keeps the note path the session already has open: the shared planner returns that session's existing `status: open` note and only otherwise builds a timestamped path.\n    42\t\n    43\tBoth `prepare` and `claim` require `--session <id>`. Only Claude can use `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` instead. `CLAUDE_PID` supplies the runtime process id for the shell-child check only. Runtimes without a session-id variable generate an id with `uuidgen` and reuse it for the session.\n    44\t\n    45\tPrepare validates the note before checking whether the caller runs under skilled-loop. The nudge passes `--runtime <name>`, which must name one of the seven runtimes. Outside the loop, that value overrides the environment or Claude default and the CLI prints the selected runtime's manual claim command before exiting 3. Inside the loop, a supplied runtime must match `SKILLED_LOOP_RUNTIME` or prepare exits 2. A live child of the runtime process also exits 2 when its command name is one of `sh`, `bash`, `zsh`, `fish`, `dash`, `ksh`, `mksh`, `tcsh`, `csh`, `nu` or `pwsh`, with a leading `-` stripped so a login shell still matches. Any other child program is not detected. A successful prepare below the hop cap writes a pending record and exits 0. At the cap it writes no record: it prints a `hop limit reached` line that names auto-compaction as the fallback, then the manual claim command, logs `prepare-declined` with reason `hop-cap` and exits 3, leaving the note open for the operator's own claim. When a hop goes ahead, prepare marks each note already claimed by this session as `done`, adds `completed_at` and sets `superseded_by` to the new note's basename.\n    46\t\n    47\tThe note's Remaining work lists only unfinished task work. Note creation, continuity saving and prepare stay outside that section. Claude saves a bound packet with `/speckit:save`. Other runtimes pipe the save JSON to `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js --stdin --full-auto <spec-folder>`. Turn-end arming matches the event session id to the prepared record. Missing required payload data or background work leaves it pending. Otherwise, the adapter writes `status: armed` before starting the detached killer. A killer spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next arm event restarts its killer. Every arm path restarts a killer for an armed record with no live killer owner. It does nothing while one is alive.\n    48\t\n    49\t### Killer And Wrapper\n    50\t\n    51\tThe killer polls transcript modification time every 100 ms. Once the transcript is idle, it confirms that the target descends from the loop and re-reads the record. It sends SIGTERM only when the record is armed. Before a signal it re-checks the hop cap: a record at the cap is un-armed with reason `hop-cap`, logged as `killer-hop-cap` and left unsignalled with no notification, because no relaunch would follow and the manual claim is already the session's own instruction. Without a transcript it treats elapsed idle time since start as the idle signal. In OpenCode's Bun-hosted plugin, the detached killer runs with node because process.execPath points to the OpenCode CLI. A single failed transcript read does not end supervision. The killer gives up only after the transcript has stayed unreadable for 1 s without a break. That give-up re-reads the record first. When a later prepare armed a different note, it leaves that record alone and logs `killer-timeout-superseded` with the `watched` and `current` note paths. Otherwise it un-arms the record with reason killer-timeout and attempts the notification. Taking over a stale killer lock goes through a takeover mutex that records its owner pid. The mutex is reclaimed when that owner is dead. It is also reclaimed once it has outlived the longest hold a real holder needs. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s.\n    52\t\n    53\tSESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS and SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_AGE_MIN both accept zero. Zero max hops declines every relaunch with reason `hop-cap`. Zero max age declines an armed record once its age is greater than zero. The hooks, wrapper and killer append events to relaunch.log with fsync at mode 0600. A new event starts on its own line after a partial last line, so concurrent writers keep one event per line. A corrupt relaunch file is logged and treated as absent, so should-relaunch declines with reason no-file. The command also treats EACCES, ENOTDIR or EISDIR while reading the record as absent. Other command errors exit 2. Context transcript and settings read errors return absent values instead of escaping as exceptions.\n    54\t\n    55\tskilled-loop defaults to Claude when it receives no arguments or a prompt without a runtime name. The `cursor` arm launches the `cursor-agent` binary. It preserves the first launch arguments, including name flags. It scans name options only until --. Across hops, it carries model selection for all runtimes, the provider for Pi and Hermes, and Pi extension-selection flags. The wrapper applies the same value check to model, name, Pi extension and Pi or Hermes provider options. It leaves a value equal to `--` or starting with `-` in the first launch's arguments and does not carry it into later hops. Relative Pi extension paths are resolved against the wrapper's starting directory. Claude and Pi are the only runtimes with a launch-time name flag.\n    56\t\n    57\tA matching armed record is consumed only when it is fresh and below the hop cap. `spec_folder` carries three states: a repo-relative packet path, `none` when Gate 3 was answered D) Skip or empty when Gate 3 was never answered. The wrapper adds the path or `none` to the claim seed. The receiver resumes a packet path, skips Gate 3 for `none` and asks Gate 3 when the field is empty. The seed also carries the note path, so every adapter's claim instructions name the real file rather than leaving the `<handoff-path>` placeholder. Seeds printed by earlier builds spell the Skip suffix differently. The receiver still reads them as Skip. AGENTS.md states that a relaunch hop is the same session. The receiving runtime claims the note and continues only its task work. It never runs prepare on an already claimed note.\n    58\t\n    59\t`SESSION_RELAUNCH_THRESHOLD` controls when each session is nudged. Keep it well above a fresh session's startup fill plus its remaining work. Codex live runs chained hop after hop at `0.12` because each fresh session reached about 10% on startup alone. `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` caps completed relaunches. At the cap the chain ends on its own: prepare writes no record, the adapters stop nudging and the killer un-arms an armed record, so a re-nudged receiver is never killed for a relaunch that cannot start. Use a low hop cap for live checks.\n    60\t\n    61\t### Pi And OpenCode Adapters\n    62\t\n    63\tPi checks usage at message_end and holds the nudge text there rather than sending it, because the message that crosses the threshold usually carries the prepare call itself. agent_end re-checks the record and the hop cap, then delivers the held text with deliverAs: 'followUp', triggerTurn: true, so the nudge starts its own turn and needs no operator input. It captures a matching seed in before_agent_start and arms the Pi record on agent_end. Its killer-path resolver walks up from the current directory, so it can find killer.cjs from a repository subdirectory. tsconfig.pi.json includes the Pi extension entry for type-checking.\n    64\t\n    65\tOpenCode reads message.updated and message.part.updated. It pushes the active nudge on every request while this session has no handoff. Outside skilled-loop it keeps pushing until the handoff note exists. A matching claim seed stays in the system prompt on every request of the session until the note leaves the open state or a new non-seed user message arrives, because an auxiliary request can consume the first prompt. The seed enters through experimental.chat.system.transform. The relaunch record is armed on session.idle.\n    66\t\n    67\tThe wrapper's Pi relaunch carries provider and model selection together with -ne and each -e extension selection from the initial launch. In this repository, start Pi with -ne -e .pi/extensions/session-relaunch.ts because two installed extensions both register --fast. OpenCode is started with -m <provider/model>. The wrapper preserves that model selection across hops.\n    68\t\n    69\tIn the recorded live runs, OpenCode reported about 1M context tokens for openai/gpt-6-luna and Pi reported 272K. These reported window sizes differ by runtime.\n    70\t\n    71\t### Codex Adapter\n    72\t\n    73\tCodex binds three repo hooks rendered from hook-registry.json into .codex/hooks.json, one script per event because the Codex hook installer allows each script under one event only. `prompt-seed.mjs` on UserPromptSubmit returns claim-seed instructions for a `[session-relaunch]` prompt and runs the threshold check for any other prompt. `context-nudge.mjs` on PostToolUse runs the same check mid-session. `stop-arm.mjs` accepts only `hook_event_name: Stop`. It arms a matching pending record before spawning the killer with `--transcript <transcript_path>`, so a spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next Stop restarts its killer.\n    74\t\n    75\tThe fill reads the rollout's latest `token_count` event at `transcript_path`. The numerator is `last_token_usage.input_tokens`, which already contains the cached share, so `cached_input_tokens` is not added. The window is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the event's `model_context_window`. The nudge reaches the model as `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`. Codex exports no pid variable, so the killer target resolves through the ancestor rule the CLI's running-work check uses.\n    76\t\n    77\t### Hermes Adapter\n    78\t\n    79\tHermes runs through the enabled `repo-guards` project plugin, which bridges its plugin hooks to the Node entry points under `hermes/`. `post_api_request` records each call's `usage` per session. `pre_llm_call` returns the context adapter's seed text on a `[session-relaunch]` first turn or the handoff text once the fill crosses the threshold, appended to the turn's user message. `on_session_end` accepts a missing event name from the plugin bridge and rejects a supplied name other than `on_session_end`. It arms a pending record for this session before detaching the killer with the Hermes process resolved by the ancestor rule, so a spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next session end restarts its killer.\n    80\t\n    81\tHermes fires `on_session_end` after every message, so the plugin arms there and keeps each session's recorded usage until `on_session_finalize` or `on_session_reset` clears it. The fill counts input plus cache read and cache write tokens, never output. The window is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the context length in the event. If the event has no window, the plugin asks Hermes model metadata for the model window. The 200-thousand-token default is used only when neither source has a value. The chosen source is logged.\n    82\t\n    83\t### Command-Only Runtimes\n    84\t\n    85\tCursor ships on the command trigger because its evidence is source-only and `stop` is not wired in this repository's `.cursor/hooks.json`. Devin ships on the command trigger because its hook usage is unverified. On both, `/speckit:handoff` writes the note and runs prepare. Outside skilled-loop, prepare prints one runtime-specific manual claim command and exits 3. A prepared record stays pending because no arm event exists for these runtimes. When the runtime exits with a pending record, the wrapper also prints its manual claim command and preserves the runtime exit code. When a path contains `!`, the printed seed places the bang in single quotes outside the double quotes, as in `/tmp/han\"'!'\"doff.md`. Interactive Bash keeps the path intact when history expansion is on. If the wrapper cannot decode the record returned by `should-relaunch`, it prints `warning: could not read the relaunch record`, consumes the record and exits 1 without a manual claim command.\n    86\t\n    87\t---\n    88\t## 3. SOURCE FILES\n    89\t\n    90\t### Implementation\n    91\t\n    92\t| File | Layer | Role |\n    93\t|---|---|---|\n    94\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/claude/context-nudge.mjs | Handler | Reads hook input and returns threshold or claim-seed context. |\n    95\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/claude/stop-arm.mjs | Handler | Arms a matching prepared record on Stop and starts the killer. |\n    96\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/codex/prompt-seed.mjs | Handler | Answers `[session-relaunch]` seeds and runs the nudge check on UserPromptSubmit. |\n    97\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/codex/context-nudge.mjs | Handler | Runs the rollout-usage threshold check on PostToolUse. |\n    98\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/codex/shared.mjs | Shared | Shared payload, threshold and emit logic for the Codex entry points. |\n    99\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/codex/stop-arm.mjs | Handler | Arms a matching pending record on Stop and starts the killer with its transcript. |\n   100\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/hermes/context-nudge.mjs | Handler | Returns seed or handoff context for the plugin's pre_llm_call bridge. |\n   101\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/hermes/session-end-arm.mjs | Handler | Arms a matching pending record on on_session_end and starts the killer. |\n   102\t| .hermes/plugins/repo-guards/__init__.py | Handler | Bridges Hermes plugin hooks to the adapters and keeps per-session usage. |\n   103\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/pi/session-relaunch.ts | Handler | Sends context nudges, injects a relaunch seed, arms Pi records and resolves the killer path from repository subdirectories. |\n   104\t| .opencode/plugins/session-relaunch.js | Handler | Queues OpenCode nudge or seed text for a system transform and arms a matching record on idle. |\n   105\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/bin/session-relaunch.cjs | Script | Validates notes, prepares records and updates note status. |\n   106\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/context-usage.cjs | Shared | Reads Claude and Codex transcript usage, calculates Pi, OpenCode and Hermes token fills and resolves context windows. |\n   107\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/nudge.cjs | Shared | Builds the nudge and seed text, parses the note path, threshold and Gate 3 seed suffix. It also owns per-session level state. |\n   108\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/relaunch-core.cjs | Shared | Validates handoffs and handles relaunch and note state. |\n   109\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs | Script | Waits for transcript idle, gives up after a 1 s unreadable transcript, signals the target and guards stale-lock takeover. |\n   110\t| .skilled/bin/skilled-loop | Script | Wraps the seven runtimes and launches eligible relaunches with runtime-specific seed arguments. |\n   111\t| tsconfig.pi.json | TypeScript config | Includes the Pi extension entry so the adapter is type-checked. |\n   112\t| .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/hook-registry.json | Shared | Declares the Claude and Codex hook bindings rendered into each runtime's config. |\n   113\t\n   114\t### Validation And Tests\n   115\t\n   116\t| File | Type | Role |\n   117\t|---|---|---|\n   118\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/claude-adapters.test.cjs | Node test | Checks Claude nudge and Stop adapter behavior. |\n   119\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/codex-adapters.test.cjs | Node test | Checks Codex seed, threshold, arming and registration behavior. |\n   120\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/hermes-adapters.test.cjs | Node test | Checks Hermes usage, seed, window sources, arming and pid lookup. |\n   121\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/nudge.test.cjs | Node test | Checks the shared nudge text, levels and seed parsing. |\n   122\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/pi/session-relaunch.test.ts | Vitest | Checks Pi nudge, seed injection and agent-end arming. |\n   123\t| .opencode/plugins/tests/session-relaunch.test.cjs | Node test | Checks OpenCode event handling, transform injection and idle arming. |\n   124\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/context-usage.test.cjs | Node test | Checks transcript parsing, runtime usage calculations and window resolution. |\n   125\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/killer.test.cjs | Node test | Checks process supervision, timeout and lock behavior. |\n   126\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/relaunch-core.test.cjs | Node test | Checks note states and relaunch decisions. |\n   127\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/session-relaunch-cli.test.cjs | Node test | Checks prepare, claim, complete and CLI refusal paths. |\n   128\t| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/skilled-loop.test.cjs | Node test | Checks wrapper relaunch and per-runtime seed arguments with stub runtimes. |\n   129\t| .hermes/plugins/repo-guards/tests/test_repo_guards.py | Automated test | Checks the plugin bridge, including the relaunch context and arm paths. |\n   130\t\n   131\t---\n   132\t\n   133\t## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n   134\t\n   135\t- Group: Context Preservation\n   136\t- Canonical catalog source: feature-catalog.md\n   137\t- Feature file path: context-preservation/session-relaunch-loop.md\n   138\t\n   139\tRelated references:\n   140\t- [Resource map template](resource-map-template.md)\n=== benchmark README row\n27:| 2026-10-19 | [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/`](./2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/) | claude, pi, opencode session-relaunch with no Gate 3 answer | 3 PASS | **PASS** | `manual-testing-playbook` |\n28:| 2026-10-19 | [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/`](./2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/) | claude, pi, opencode, codex session-relaunch | 4 PASS | **PASS** | `manual-testing-playbook` |\n29:| 2026-10-19 | [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/`](./2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/) | claude, pi, opencode, codex session-relaunch | 11 PASS, 2 FAIL | **PASS (after fixes)** | `manual-testing-playbook` |\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cat -n .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/context-preservation/session-relaunch-loop.md | sed -n '1,140p'; echo \"=== benchmark README row\"; grep -n \"session-relaunch-gate-exemptions\\|session-relaunch-final-code\\|session-relaunch-live-four-cli\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/benchmark/reports/README.md | head","time":{"start":1792422009271,"end":1792422009304}},"id":"prt_154ad57fe001pldmOGoh3M8TO1","sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","messageID":"msg_154ac43ac001llVtgo4WSRZQtP"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792422009804,"sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01_UwdfxHG4nvknL5I6jT269525","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"for f in .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-claude.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-pi-opencode.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-command-runtimes.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; grep -n \"2026-10-19\\|run 10\\|gate-exemptions\" \"$f\" | head -6; done","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/148-session-relaunch-deep-review"},"output":"=== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-claude.md\n46:Three 2026-10-19 benchmark reports hold the outcomes, operator inputs and evidence paths for this live check: [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/README.md) records the final-code round, [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/README.md) records the earlier runs made while the gaps were being fixed, and [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/README.md) records the run with no Gate 3 answer.\n=== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-pi-opencode.md\n118:The 2026-10-19 benchmark reports for this scenario hold the outcomes, operator inputs and evidence paths: [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/README.md) records the final-code round, and [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/README.md) records the earlier runs made while the gaps were being fixed. A third record, [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/README.md), covers the run with no Gate 3 answer on Pi and OpenCode.\n133:Observe Pi's `message_end` nudge, the seed injection at `before_agent_start` and matching-record arming at `agent_end`. Pi only decides the nudge at `message_end` and holds the text until `agent_end`, which re-checks the record and the hop limit before sending it as a follow-up that starts a turn. Confirm the killer ends the current Pi process and `skilled-loop` starts Pi with the prepared claim seed. Confirm the receiving TUI claims that note and continues the task. The 2026-10-19 benchmark report for this scenario records the seed claims those runs made in the receiving TUI.\n148:Observe `message.updated` usage handling, `message.part.updated` seed capture and the system transform. The claim instructions stay in the system prompt for every request until the note leaves `open` or a new non-seed user message arrives, so an auxiliary request that consumes the first copy no longer drops them. Confirm a matching record arms on `session.idle`, the killer ends the current OpenCode process and the wrapper restarts it with `--prompt \"<seed>\"` and no name flag. Confirm the receiving TUI claims the note and continues the task. The 2026-10-19 benchmark report for this scenario records the claim and completion those runs reached. `session.idle` on TUI quit remains UNVERIFIED.\n=== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-command-runtimes.md\n94:Two 2026-10-19 benchmark reports hold the outcomes, operator inputs and evidence paths for the Codex live check: [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/README.md) records the final-code round, and [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/README.md) records the earlier runs made while the gaps were being fixed. A third record, [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/README.md), covers the run with no Gate 3 answer on Claude, Pi and OpenCode, not Codex. Codex and Devin passed live checks. Hermes and Cursor were not run by operator decision.\n","metadata":{"output":"=== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-claude.md\n46:Three 2026-10-19 benchmark reports hold the outcomes, operator inputs and evidence paths for this live check: [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/README.md) records the final-code round, [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/README.md) records the earlier runs made while the gaps were being fixed, and [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/README.md) records the run with no Gate 3 answer.\n=== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-pi-opencode.md\n118:The 2026-10-19 benchmark reports for this scenario hold the outcomes, operator inputs and evidence paths: [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/README.md) records the final-code round, and [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/README.md) records the earlier runs made while the gaps were being fixed. A third record, [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/README.md), covers the run with no Gate 3 answer on Pi and OpenCode.\n133:Observe Pi's `message_end` nudge, the seed injection at `before_agent_start` and matching-record arming at `agent_end`. Pi only decides the nudge at `message_end` and holds the text until `agent_end`, which re-checks the record and the hop limit before sending it as a follow-up that starts a turn. Confirm the killer ends the current Pi process and `skilled-loop` starts Pi with the prepared claim seed. Confirm the receiving TUI claims that note and continues the task. The 2026-10-19 benchmark report for this scenario records the seed claims those runs made in the receiving TUI.\n148:Observe `message.updated` usage handling, `message.part.updated` seed capture and the system transform. The claim instructions stay in the system prompt for every request until the note leaves `open` or a new non-seed user message arrives, so an auxiliary request that consumes the first copy no longer drops them. Confirm a matching record arms on `session.idle`, the killer ends the current OpenCode process and the wrapper restarts it with `--prompt \"<seed>\"` and no name flag. Confirm the receiving TUI claims the note and continues the task. The 2026-10-19 benchmark report for this scenario records the claim and completion those runs reached. `session.idle` on TUI quit remains UNVERIFIED.\n=== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-command-runtimes.md\n94:Two 2026-10-19 benchmark reports hold the outcomes, operator inputs and evidence paths for the Codex live check: [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-final-code/README.md) records the final-code round, and [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-live-four-cli/README.md) records the earlier runs made while the gaps were being fixed. A third record, [`2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/`](../../benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/README.md), covers the run with no Gate 3 answer on Claude, Pi and OpenCode, not Codex. Codex and Devin passed live checks. Hermes and Cursor were not run by operator decision.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"for f in .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-claude.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-pi-opencode.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-command-runtimes.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; grep -n \"2026-10-19\\|run 10\\|gate-exemptions\" \"$f\" | head -6; done","time":{"start":1792422009772,"end":1792422009803}},"id":"prt_154ad59c1001lshiQFhkAtvPqu","sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","messageID":"msg_154ac43ac001llVtgo4WSRZQtP"}}
