{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1792422016167,"sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","part":{"id":"prt_154ad74a500180JpmiGOm8S5K7","messageID":"msg_154ad66b3001SlqpdKqOwg00Xl","sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","snapshot":"9ce4bfe80b0613f1c3dd4c1b5b70a1761eb45769","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792422029703,"sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_0Ff4pYjNVlvxJ5EJEfnR0721","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"stale-\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/148-session-relaunch-deep-review"},"output":"234:  const stalePath = `${lockPath}.stale-${process.pid}-${randomUUID()}`;\n293:  const prefix = `${path.basename(lockPath)}.takeover.stale-`;\n418:    const staleMutexPath = `${mutexPath}.stale-${process.pid}-${randomUUID()}`;\n","metadata":{"output":"234:  const stalePath = `${lockPath}.stale-${process.pid}-${randomUUID()}`;\n293:  const prefix = `${path.basename(lockPath)}.takeover.stale-`;\n418:    const staleMutexPath = `${mutexPath}.stale-${process.pid}-${randomUUID()}`;\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"stale-\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs","time":{"start":1792422029684,"end":1792422029699}},"id":"prt_154ada867001wiWumUaVMJ5lQ1","sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","messageID":"msg_154ad66b3001SlqpdKqOwg00Xl"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792422030312,"sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01_TVOVf4emLsvcL2aAO4aT1037","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn \"stale.*lock\\|lock.*stale\\|\\.stale-\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/context-preservation/session-relaunch-loop.md | head -20","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/148-session-relaunch-deep-review"},"output":".skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:103:The killer lock stores its owner pid, a creation timestamp and an expiry timestamp. The expiry is the idle timeout plus 5000 ms of signal wait, 3000 ms of notification time and a 60000 ms margin. A lock past its expiry, or a two-field lock from an earlier build that is older than 10 minutes, does not count as a live owner even while its pid exists, because that pid may by then belong to an unrelated process. A live owner or a lock younger than five seconds without a readable owner pid logs `killer-lock-held`. Taking over a stale lock first acquires a takeover mutex, the `<lock>.takeover` directory, whose `owner` file records the pid of the killer that holds it. The mutex is reclaimed only when that owner is dead. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s. The reclaim is an atomic rename. A live holder keeps exclusion only while the directory stays younger than the 10-minute hold bound, because a real holder holds the mutex for a re-check, an unlink and a create, never minutes. Past that bound the recorded pid counts as reused and the mutex is reclaimed. A killer that cannot acquire the mutex logs `killer-lock-held`. The mutex holder re-checks the lock, logs `killer-lock-stale`, removes the lock and retries once. Leftover `.stale-` takeover directories older than 30 s are swept under the mutex. The killer records the inode of the mutex directory it creates and, on release, removes the path only while that inode still matches, so it never removes a takeover directory it did not create. The killer releases the lock file the same way: it removes the file only while the recorded owner pid is its own, and logs `killer-lock-not-owned` when a contender has taken it over. An owner file that cannot be read fails the killer with `killer-error` and exit code 1. The exceptions are a non-directory at the mutex path, which has no owner file and is judged by age, and an owner read that fails with `ENOENT` or `EACCES`, which counts as no owner. If releasing the mutex fails after the takeover has finished, the killer logs `killer-mutex-release-error` with the error code. The takeover result stands: the killer keeps the lock it acquired and continues supervising.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:143:The hook keeps per-session nudge levels in `nudge-<session-id>.json` in the same runtime state directory. The hooks and wrapper append events to `relaunch.log` on a best-effort basis. The killer appends its events there too. A failed append in the killer is not swallowed. Each event is one line, appended under fsync at mode 0600, with the mode re-pinned on every append. When the previous last line has no newline, as after a partial write, the new event starts on its own line, so concurrent writers keep one event per line. A short write, from a full disk or a file-size limit, throws `relaunch log short write: wrote X of Y bytes to <path>` instead of losing the event quietly. The killer lock file stores the owner pid, the creation timestamp and the expiry stamp. A lock past its expiry counts as stale, so a reused pid cannot keep the loop pinned. Active or newly created locks log `killer-lock-held`. Stale locks log `killer-lock-stale` before the killer removes and retries them.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:183:If the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`, it found no armed record before signalling. `killer-hop-cap` means the record's hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS`, so the killer unarmed it with reason `hop-cap` and sent no signal. `killer-lock-held` means another killer owns the lock or is creating it. `killer-lock-stale` means the owner process is gone or the unreadable lock is older than five seconds. `killer-lock-not-owned` means the lock no longer named this killer's pid when it exited, because a stall past the expiry let a contender take it over, so the killer left the file in place for its new owner. `target-gone` marks a target that exited between the final liveness check and SIGTERM. The killer polls every 100 ms. The idle timeout defaults to 30000 ms and the separate post-signal grace period is 5000 ms. If either wait expires while Claude remains alive, the killer disarms the record and logs `killer-timeout`. A transcript that stays unreadable for 1 s also ends with `killer-timeout`. `killer-timeout-superseded` replaces it when a later prepare armed a different note during the wait. The killer then logs the `watched` and `current` note paths and neither disarms nor notifies, because the newer note still needs its own killer. A signal probe that fails with `EPERM` means the pid belongs to another user's process, so the killer treats its target as gone instead of signalling it. `killer-lock-held` also appears while another killer holds the `<lock>.takeover` mutex. If an armed record has no live killer, the next arm event starts one. A live killer that owns the lock blocks that restart by design. `killer-error` with exit code 1 means the takeover mutex's owner file could not be read, for example because the owner path is a directory. The killer stops before the takeover and the armed record stays armed for the next arm event. `killer-mutex-release-error` means the `<lock>.takeover` directory could not be removed after the takeover finished. The takeover result stands and the killer keeps the lock it acquired, so this line does not mean the handoff failed. A leftover mutex is reclaimed by the next takeover once its owner is dead, or once it has no owner file and is older than 30 s.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/context-preservation/session-relaunch-loop.md:51:The killer polls transcript modification time every 100 ms. Once the transcript is idle, it confirms that the target descends from the loop and re-reads the record. It sends SIGTERM only when the record is armed. Before a signal it re-checks the hop cap: a record at the cap is un-armed with reason `hop-cap`, logged as `killer-hop-cap` and left unsignalled with no notification, because no relaunch would follow and the manual claim is already the session's own instruction. Without a transcript it treats elapsed idle time since start as the idle signal. In OpenCode's Bun-hosted plugin, the detached killer runs with node because process.execPath points to the OpenCode CLI. A single failed transcript read does not end supervision. The killer gives up only after the transcript has stayed unreadable for 1 s without a break. That give-up re-reads the record first. When a later prepare armed a different note, it leaves that record alone and logs `killer-timeout-superseded` with the `watched` and `current` note paths. Otherwise it un-arms the record with reason killer-timeout and attempts the notification. Taking over a stale killer lock goes through a takeover mutex that records its owner pid. The mutex is reclaimed when that owner is dead. It is also reclaimed once it has outlived the longest hold a real holder needs. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/context-preservation/session-relaunch-loop.md:109:| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs | Script | Waits for transcript idle, gives up after a 1 s unreadable transcript, signals the target and guards stale-lock takeover. |\n","metadata":{"output":".skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:103:The killer lock stores its owner pid, a creation timestamp and an expiry timestamp. The expiry is the idle timeout plus 5000 ms of signal wait, 3000 ms of notification time and a 60000 ms margin. A lock past its expiry, or a two-field lock from an earlier build that is older than 10 minutes, does not count as a live owner even while its pid exists, because that pid may by then belong to an unrelated process. A live owner or a lock younger than five seconds without a readable owner pid logs `killer-lock-held`. Taking over a stale lock first acquires a takeover mutex, the `<lock>.takeover` directory, whose `owner` file records the pid of the killer that holds it. The mutex is reclaimed only when that owner is dead. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s. The reclaim is an atomic rename. A live holder keeps exclusion only while the directory stays younger than the 10-minute hold bound, because a real holder holds the mutex for a re-check, an unlink and a create, never minutes. Past that bound the recorded pid counts as reused and the mutex is reclaimed. A killer that cannot acquire the mutex logs `killer-lock-held`. The mutex holder re-checks the lock, logs `killer-lock-stale`, removes the lock and retries once. Leftover `.stale-` takeover directories older than 30 s are swept under the mutex. The killer records the inode of the mutex directory it creates and, on release, removes the path only while that inode still matches, so it never removes a takeover directory it did not create. The killer releases the lock file the same way: it removes the file only while the recorded owner pid is its own, and logs `killer-lock-not-owned` when a contender has taken it over. An owner file that cannot be read fails the killer with `killer-error` and exit code 1. The exceptions are a non-directory at the mutex path, which has no owner file and is judged by age, and an owner read that fails with `ENOENT` or `EACCES`, which counts as no owner. If releasing the mutex fails after the takeover has finished, the killer logs `killer-mutex-release-error` with the error code. The takeover result stands: the killer keeps the lock it acquired and continues supervising.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:143:The hook keeps per-session nudge levels in `nudge-<session-id>.json` in the same runtime state directory. The hooks and wrapper append events to `relaunch.log` on a best-effort basis. The killer appends its events there too. A failed append in the killer is not swallowed. Each event is one line, appended under fsync at mode 0600, with the mode re-pinned on every append. When the previous last line has no newline, as after a partial write, the new event starts on its own line, so concurrent writers keep one event per line. A short write, from a full disk or a file-size limit, throws `relaunch log short write: wrote X of Y bytes to <path>` instead of losing the event quietly. The killer lock file stores the owner pid, the creation timestamp and the expiry stamp. A lock past its expiry counts as stale, so a reused pid cannot keep the loop pinned. Active or newly created locks log `killer-lock-held`. Stale locks log `killer-lock-stale` before the killer removes and retries them.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:183:If the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`, it found no armed record before signalling. `killer-hop-cap` means the record's hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS`, so the killer unarmed it with reason `hop-cap` and sent no signal. `killer-lock-held` means another killer owns the lock or is creating it. `killer-lock-stale` means the owner process is gone or the unreadable lock is older than five seconds. `killer-lock-not-owned` means the lock no longer named this killer's pid when it exited, because a stall past the expiry let a contender take it over, so the killer left the file in place for its new owner. `target-gone` marks a target that exited between the final liveness check and SIGTERM. The killer polls every 100 ms. The idle timeout defaults to 30000 ms and the separate post-signal grace period is 5000 ms. If either wait expires while Claude remains alive, the killer disarms the record and logs `killer-timeout`. A transcript that stays unreadable for 1 s also ends with `killer-timeout`. `killer-timeout-superseded` replaces it when a later prepare armed a different note during the wait. The killer then logs the `watched` and `current` note paths and neither disarms nor notifies, because the newer note still needs its own killer. A signal probe that fails with `EPERM` means the pid belongs to another user's process, so the killer treats its target as gone instead of signalling it. `killer-lock-held` also appears while another killer holds the `<lock>.takeover` mutex. If an armed record has no live killer, the next arm event starts one. A live killer that owns the lock blocks that restart by design. `killer-error` with exit code 1 means the takeover mutex's owner file could not be read, for example because the owner path is a directory. The killer stops before the takeover and the armed record stays armed for the next arm event. `killer-mutex-release-error` means the `<lock>.takeover` directory could not be removed after the takeover finished. The takeover result stands and the killer keeps the lock it acquired, so this line does not mean the handoff failed. A leftover mutex is reclaimed by the next takeover once its owner is dead, or once it has no owner file and is older than 30 s.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/context-preservation/session-relaunch-loop.md:51:The killer polls transcript modification time every 100 ms. Once the transcript is idle, it confirms that the target descends from the loop and re-reads the record. It sends SIGTERM only when the record is armed. Before a signal it re-checks the hop cap: a record at the cap is un-armed with reason `hop-cap`, logged as `killer-hop-cap` and left unsignalled with no notification, because no relaunch would follow and the manual claim is already the session's own instruction. Without a transcript it treats elapsed idle time since start as the idle signal. In OpenCode's Bun-hosted plugin, the detached killer runs with node because process.execPath points to the OpenCode CLI. A single failed transcript read does not end supervision. The killer gives up only after the transcript has stayed unreadable for 1 s without a break. That give-up re-reads the record first. When a later prepare armed a different note, it leaves that record alone and logs `killer-timeout-superseded` with the `watched` and `current` note paths. Otherwise it un-arms the record with reason killer-timeout and attempts the notification. Taking over a stale killer lock goes through a takeover mutex that records its owner pid. The mutex is reclaimed when that owner is dead. It is also reclaimed once it has outlived the longest hold a real holder needs. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/context-preservation/session-relaunch-loop.md:109:| .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs | Script | Waits for transcript idle, gives up after a 1 s unreadable transcript, signals the target and guards stale-lock takeover. |\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rn \"stale.*lock\\|lock.*stale\\|\\.stale-\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/context-preservation/session-relaunch-loop.md | head -20","time":{"start":1792422030297,"end":1792422030311}},"id":"prt_154ada975001iZNqpMZi8XtlVx","sessionID":"ses_eab5d5530ffemUeXMVtsM2xDB5","messageID":"msg_154ad66b3001SlqpdKqOwg00Xl"}}
